(2688, '25-02-103-M2688', 59, 103, 355, 1, 10, '10', 1800.00, '2025-09-11', 1, NULL, 'The Oil and Gas sector operates in a highly complex, capital-intensive, and risk-driven environment that demands strong managerial capabilities, strategic thinking, and effective leadership. Rapid technological change, fluctuating energy markets, stringent safety requirements, and global competition require managers who are adaptable, data-driven, and performance-oriented.\nThis technical proposal presents a 10-day Modern Management Training Program designed to enhance the managerial effectiveness of professionals working in oil and gas companies by equipping them with modern management tools, leadership skills, and best practices aligned with international standards.\n', 'The main objectives of this training program are to:\n•	Strengthen modern management and leadership competencies in the oil and gas context\n•	Enhance strategic thinking and decision-making skills\n•	Improve operational efficiency and performance management\n•	Develop effective communication, team leadership, and problem-solving abilities\n•	Introduce modern management tools, techniques, and global best practices\n•	Support participants in managing change, risk, and organizational performance\n', 'This program is designed for:\n•	Mid-level and senior managers\n•	Supervisors and team leaders\n•	Project managers\n•	Department heads and section leaders\n•	Technical professionals transitioning into management roles\n', 'Day 1: Modern Management Concepts\n\nEvolution of management theories\nTraditional vs. modern management\nManagement challenges in the oil and gas sector\nManagerial roles and competencies\n\nDay 2: Strategic Management\n\nStrategic thinking and planning\nVision, mission, and strategic objectives\nSWOT and PESTLE analysis\nStrategy implementation in oil and gas organizations\n\nDay 3: Leadership in Oil & Gas Organizations\n\nLeadership styles and approaches\nSituational and transformational leadership\nLeading technical and multicultural teams\nEthical leadership and corporate governance\n\nDay 4: Organizational Behavior & Team Management\n\nUnderstanding human behavior at work\nMotivation theories and engagement\nBuilding high-performance teams\nManaging conflict and diversity\n\nDay 5: Communication & Stakeholder Management\n\nEffective managerial communication\nPresentation and reporting skills\nNegotiation techniques\nInternal and external stakeholder management\n\nDay 6: Performance Management & KPIs\n\nPerformance management systems\nKey Performance Indicators (KPIs)\nMonitoring, evaluation, and reporting\nContinuous improvement practices\n\nDay 7: Project Management Fundamentals\n\nProject management principles\nPlanning, scheduling, and control\nRisk, cost, and quality management\nProject management in oil and gas operations\n\nDay 8: Decision-Making & Problem Solving\n\nAnalytical and critical thinking\nDecision-making models and tools\nRisk-based decision making\nManaging uncertainty and complexity\n\nDay 9: Change Management & Innovation\n\nManaging organizational change\nResistance to change\nInnovation and continuous improvement\nDigital transformation in oil and gas\n\nDay 10: Health, Safety, Environment & Management Integration\n\nHSE culture and leadership\nIntegrating HSE into management systems\nCrisis and incident management\nProgram review, action planning, and evaluation'),
(2689, '25-02-103-C2689', 59, 103, 356, 5, 10, '10', 0.00, '2025-09-12', 1, NULL, 'This 10-day hands-on training program provides participants with a comprehensive understanding of Microsoft’s modern security ecosystem, spanning Defender XDR, Microsoft Sentinel, and Copilot for Security Operations. The course is designed to build both foundational and advanced skills in threat detection, investigation, hunting, and automated response.\nParticipants will start with core SOC concepts and Microsoft cloud security services, then progressively move into incident response, analytics rule creation, automation with SOAR, and finally leverage AI-driven Copilot capabilities to accelerate investigations, write KQL queries, and deliver executive-level reporting.', 'By the end of this training, participants will be able to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand SOC operations and Microsoft’s security ecosystem, including Zero Trust principles.\r\n\r\nConfigure and manage Microsoft Defender XDR for endpoint, identity, email, and cloud app protection.\r\n\r\nInvestigate security incidents using Defender XDR’s automated and manual tools.\r\n\r\nApply Kusto Query Language (KQL) for advanced hunting and analytics across Defender and Sentinel.\r\n\r\nDeploy and configure Microsoft Sentinel workspaces, connectors, and log analytics.\r\n\r\nCreate and fine-tune analytics rules for real-world attack detection.\r\n\r\nImplement automation rules and incident response playbooks using SOAR workflows.\r\n\r\nLeverage Copilot for Security to generate prompts, assist investigations, and produce compliance-ready reports.\r\n\r\nPerform end-to-end investigations and responses through a practical capstone lab simulating real-world SOC scenarios.', 'This course is designed for:\r\n\r\nSOC Analysts (Tier 1–3) seeking to strengthen their investigation, hunting, and incident response skills.\r\n\r\nSecurity Engineers and Administrators responsible for configuring and managing Microsoft Defender XDR and Sentinel.\r\n\r\nIT and Cloud Security Professionals aiming to gain hands-on expertise in Microsoft’s security ecosystem.\r\n\r\nThreat Hunters and Incident Responders who need advanced KQL and automation capabilities.\r\n\r\nCybersecurity Managers and Team Leads interested in leveraging AI-driven tools (Copilot) for faster decision-making and reporting.', 'Day 1 – Security and Microsoft Environment Foundations\r\n•	SOC Basics: Role and functions of a Security Operations Center.\r\n•	CTI Basics: Cyber Threat Intelligence essentials.\r\n•	Vulnerabilities Basics: Identification, prioritization, mitigation.\r\n•	Azure and Microsoft Cloud Services basics.\r\n•	Active Directory, RAS, DMZ, and Virtualization concepts.\r\n•	Microsoft Security ecosystem and Zero Trust model.\r\nDay 2 – XDR Configuration\r\n•	Setting up Microsoft Defender XDR environment.\r\n•	Licensing, onboarding devices, enabling (Endpoint – Office – Identity - Cloud Apps).\r\n•	Role based access control, device groups, and policies.\r\n•	Secure Score configuration for continuous assessment.\r\n•	Configure Defender portal and onboard a device.\r\nDay 3 – XDR Incident Investigation\r\n•	Navigating incidents and alerts in Defender XDR.\r\n•	Automated investigations and response actions.\r\n•	Threat Analytics and vulnerability insights.\r\n•	 Simulate and investigate (phishing - email threats).\r\nDay 4 – XDR Threat Hunting\r\n•	Introduction to Kusto Query Language (KQL) in Defender.\r\n•	Building advanced hunting queries.\r\n•	Creating and tuning custom detections.\r\n•	Proactive hunting across endpoints, email, and identities.\r\n•	Build dashboards for hunting.\r\nDay 5 – Sentinel Workspace Setup\r\n•	Sentinel architecture and deployment.\r\n•	Creating and configuring Sentinel workspaces.\r\n•	Connecting Defender XDR, Azure, Syslog - CEF data sources.\r\n•	Managing log retention and Sentinel roles.\r\n•	Workspace setup with Defender integration.\r\nDay 6 – KQL Queries in Sentinel\r\n•	Writing and refining KQL queries for log analytics.\r\n•	Hunting queries from Content Hub.\r\n•	Customizing queries for real world attack detection.\r\n•	Create hunting queries for brute force and lateral movement.\r\nDay 7 – Analytics Rules\r\n•	Configuring scheduled, near-real-time, and fusion rules.\r\n•	UEBA (User and Entity Behavior Analytics) for anomaly detection.\r\n•	Classifying and correlating data with ASIM parsers.\r\n•	Build and test custom analytics rules.\r\nDay 8 – Automation and Playbooks\r\n•	SOAR in Sentinel: concepts and workflows.\r\n•	Creating automation rules for alert triage and suppression.\r\n•	Designing incident response playbooks with Logic Apps.\r\n•	Using watchlists and workbooks for enhanced visibility.\r\n•	Automate phishing response with playbooks.\r\nDay 9 – Prompt Engineering and KQL with Copilot\r\n•	Writing effective prompts for SOC investigations and hunting.\r\n•	Using Copilot to generate and refine KQL queries for Sentinel and XDR.\r\n•	Building reusable prompt templates for common security tasks.\r\n•	KQL queries for real detections.\r\nDay 10 – Copilot for Incident Response and Summarization\r\n•	Using Copilot for guided attack investigation and response.\r\n•	Automating containment and remediation recommendations with AI.\r\n•	Generating plain-language summaries for incidents.\r\n•	Drafting executive and compliance-ready reports with Copilot.\r\n•	Final Capstone Lab: (Defender alert investigation - Sentinel correlation with KQL - Copilot-guided response and report).\r\n'),
(2690, '25-02-110-F2690', 46, 110, 357, 4, 3, '4', 1650.00, '2025-09-05', 1, NULL, '', '', '', ''),
(2692, '25-02-110-C2692', 46, 110, 358, 5, 3, '4', 1650.00, '2025-09-05', 1, NULL, 'Artificial Intelligence (AI) is reshaping industries, business models, and executive decision-making across the globe. This workshop is designed specifically for executives and senior leaders who need to understand AI from a strategic, managerial, and decision-making perspective rather than technical coding details.\r\n\r\nThe program provides practical insights, use cases, tools, and frameworks that empower executives to leverage AI for competitive advantage, operational efficiency, and innovation.', 'Introduce executives to the fundamentals of Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning.\r\nProvide an overview of AI applications across various industries.\r\nEquip leaders with frameworks for AI-driven strategy and decision-making.\r\nDemonstrate case studies of successful AI adoption in business.\r\nAddress challenges such as ethics, data governance, and change management.\r\nEnable participants to draft an AI roadmap for their organizations.', 'CEOs, CTOs, CIOs, and C-Level executives.\r\nSenior managers, business unit heads & consultants\r\nDecision-makers in strategy, operations, and innovation.\r\nExecutives planning to implement AI solutions in their organizations.', 'Day 1: Understanding AI Foundations\r\nIntroduction to Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning\r\nKey AI concepts: supervised, unsupervised, deep learning\r\nAI vs Automation vs Data Analytics\r\nIndustry 4.0 and AI’s role in business transformation\r\nExecutive perspective: Why AI matters now\r\n\r\nDay 2: AI Applications & Use Cases\r\nAI in Business Functions:\r\nFinance (fraud detection, predictive analytics)\r\nMarketing & Sales (customer insights, personalization)\r\nOperations (supply chain optimization, predictive maintenance)\r\nHR & People Analytics (talent acquisition, retention)\r\nCase Studies from Fortune 500 companies\r\nGroup Activity: Mapping AI opportunities in participants’ sectors\r\n\r\nDay 3: Strategic Implementation & Governance\r\nBuilding AI Strategy: Vision, Objectives, ROI\r\nData governance & infrastructure readiness\r\nEthical, legal, and regulatory considerations in AI\r\nRisks & limitations of AI adoption\r\nChange management and workforce transformation\r\nGuest speaker session: Industry leader insights\r\n\r\nDay 4: AI Roadmap for Executives\r\nHands-on workshop: Designing AI adoption framework\r\nExecutive dashboards & KPIs for AI initiatives\r\nTools overview (AI platforms, no-code AI tools, decision-support systems)\r\nGroup presentations: Draft AI roadmaps for organizations\r\nWrap-up, feedback, and certification ceremony\r\n\r\n\r\nDeliverables\r\n4 days of interactive training by expert facilitators.\r\nWorkshop materials (presentations, handouts, toolkits).\r\nCase study compendium with real-world AI business applications.\r\nAI Roadmap template for participants.\r\nCertificates of Completion.\r\n\r\n\r\nExpected Outcomes\r\nBy the end of the workshop, executives will:\r\nUnderstand AI concepts without technical complexity.\r\nRecognize AI opportunities relevant to their industry.\r\nAssess AI readiness of their organizations.\r\nGain awareness of ethical and governance challenges.\r\nDevelop a strategic AI adoption roadmap for their business.'),
(2693, '25-02-103-C2693', 59, 103, 359, 5, 4, '10', 2150.00, '2025-09-05', 1, NULL, 'يُعد برنامج Autodesk Civil 3D أحد أقوى برامج التصميم الهندسي في مجال الهندسة المدنية، ويُستخدم بشكل واسع في أعمال البنية التحتية مثل الطرق، شبكات المياه والصرف الصحي، تصميم الأراضي (Grading) ونمذجة المعلومات الجغرافية.\r\nتهدف هذه الدورة إلى تأهيل المتدربين على استخدام البرنامج بكفاءة عالية لإنجاز المشاريع الهندسية وفق المعايير العالمية.', '•	التعرف على واجهة البرنامج وأساسيات استخدامه.\r\n•	تعلم كيفية استيراد ومعالجة النقاط (Survey Data).\r\n•	إنشاء الأسطح (Surfaces) وتحليلها.\r\n•	تصميم المحاور الأفقية (Alignments) والرأسية (Profiles).\r\n•	إعداد المقاطع العرضية (Cross Sections) والحسابات الكمية.\r\n•	تطبيقات في تصميم الطرق وشبكات البنية التحتية.\r\n•	إعداد الرسومات التنفيذية والتقارير النهائية.', '•	مهندسو مدني (طرق – بنية تحتية – مساحة).\r\n•	فنيو المساحة والرسم الهندسي.\r\n•	طلاب الهندسة المدنية في المراحل الأخيرة.\r\n•	العاملون في شركات المقاولات والمكاتب الاستشارية.', '•	مقدمة عامة عن Civil 3D\r\n•	الواجهة – Toolspace – Prospector – Settings.\r\n•	النقاط والمسح (Survey Points & Data)\r\n•	إدخال النقاط – التصنيف – إنشاء المجموعات.\r\n•	السطوح (Surfaces)\r\n•	أنواع السطوح – طرق الإنشاء – التحليل (Contours, Slopes, Volumes).\r\n•	المحاور الأفقية (Alignments)\r\n•	إنشاء الطرق والمحاور – Geometry – Curves.\r\n•	البروفايلات الرأسية (Profiles)\r\n•	إنشاء البروفايل – تعديل المناسيب – Design Profiles.\r\n•	المقاطع العرضية (Cross Sections)\r\n•	إنشاء النماذج – تصميم الطبقات – الكميات.\r\n•	تصميم الطرق (Corridors)\r\n•	Subassemblies – Assembly – Corridor Modeling.\r\n•	الشبكات (Pipes & Networks)\r\n•	تصميم شبكات مياه وصرف صحي – التحليل الهيدروليكي.\r\n•	إعداد الرسومات والتقارير\r\n•	إخراج اللوحات التنفيذية – الجداول والكميات.\r\n•	مشروع تطبيقي متكامل\r\n•	من إدخال النقاط حتى إعداد اللوحات النهائية.');
INSERT INTO `quotations` (`quot_id`, `quot_ref`, `ord_id`, `cust_id`, `course_id`, `cat_id`, `trainees`, `duration`, `cost`, `quot_date`, `ven_id`, `file_path`, `introduction`, `objectives`, `audiences`, `outlines`) VALUES
(2694, '25-02-109-E2694', 44, 109, 112, 3, 10, '10', 1850.00, '2025-09-06', 1, NULL, 'The \"Integrated Comprehensive Electrical, Mechanical, and Maintenance\" training program is designed to provide participants with a holistic understanding of electrical and mechanical systems and their maintenance in industrial settings. This two-week intensive course covers the integration of electrical and mechanical maintenance practices, focusing on optimizing the performance, reliability, and safety of complex industrial systems.', '•	Gain a comprehensive understanding of both electrical and mechanical systems in industrial environments.\r\n•	Develop skills in diagnosing, troubleshooting, and maintaining integrated electrical and mechanical systems.\r\n•	Learn how to implement preventive and predictive maintenance strategies to enhance system reliability.\r\n•	Understand the principles of system integration and how electrical and mechanical components interact.\r\n•	Enhance problem-solving abilities and decision-making skills in complex maintenance scenarios.\r\n•	Apply best practices to ensure the safety, efficiency, and longevity of industrial systems.', '•	Maintenance engineers and technicians are responsible for both electrical and mechanical systems.\r\n•	Plant operators and supervisors oversee the operation and maintenance of integrated systems.\r\n•	Electrical and mechanical engineers seeking to expand their knowledge of system integration.\r\n•	Individuals involved in developing and implementing maintenance strategies for industrial facilities.', 'Week 1: Fundamentals of Electrical and Mechanical Systems\r\nDay 1: Introduction to Integrated Maintenance\r\n•	Welcome and Program Overview: Introduction to course objectives and structure.\r\n•	Importance of Integrated Maintenance: Ensuring optimal performance of industrial systems.\r\n•	Overview of Electrical and Mechanical Systems: Key components and functions.\r\n•	Case Study: Successful integration of electrical and mechanical maintenance in industry.\r\nDay 2: Electrical Systems Fundamentals\r\n•	Understanding Electrical Circuits: Basics of AC/DC circuits, power distribution, and control systems.\r\n•	Electrical Components: Motors, transformers, relays, switches, and circuit breakers.\r\n•	Electrical Safety Practices: Protecting personnel and equipment from electrical hazards.\r\n•	Workshop: Hands-on exploration of electrical systems and components.\r\nDay 3: Mechanical Systems Fundamentals\r\n•	Understanding Mechanical Systems: Basics of hydraulics, pneumatics, and mechanical drives.\r\n•	Mechanical Components: Bearings, gears, shafts, pumps, and compressors.\r\n•	Mechanical Safety Practices: Ensuring safe operation and maintenance of mechanical systems.\r\n•	Workshop: Hands-on exploration of mechanical systems and components.\r\nDay 4: Integration of Electrical and Mechanical Systems\r\n•	Principles of System Integration: How electrical and mechanical components interact.\r\n•	Control Systems: PLCs, SCADA, and automation systems integrating electrical and mechanical components.\r\n•	Troubleshooting Integrated Systems: Techniques for diagnosing complex issues.\r\n•	Case Study: Integration challenges and solutions in an industrial setting.\r\nDay 5: Preventive and Predictive Maintenance\r\n•	Preventive Maintenance Strategies: Scheduling and performing routine maintenance tasks.\r\n•	Predictive Maintenance Techniques: Using data and analytics to anticipate failures.\r\n•	Tools for Maintenance: Vibration analysis, thermal imaging, and condition monitoring.\r\n•	Workshop: Developing a preventive and predictive maintenance plan for integrated systems.\r\nWeek 2: Advanced Maintenance Techniques and Implementation\r\nDay 6: Advanced Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\r\n•	Troubleshooting Electrical Systems: Identifying and resolving common electrical issues.\r\n•	Troubleshooting Mechanical Systems: Techniques for diagnosing mechanical failures.\r\n•	Advanced Diagnostic Tools: Using oscilloscopes, multimeters, and alignment tools.\r\n•	Workshop: Applying troubleshooting techniques to real-world scenarios.\r\nDay 7: System Reliability and Lifecycle Management\r\n•	Reliability-Centered Maintenance (RCM): Maximizing system uptime and reliability.\r\n•	Lifecycle Costing: Evaluating the total cost of ownership for electrical and mechanical systems.\r\n•	Asset Management: Strategies for extending the life of critical components.\r\n•	Case Study: Implementing RCM in an industrial environment.\r\nDay 8: Energy Efficiency and Optimization\r\n•	Energy Management: Techniques for reducing energy consumption in industrial systems.\r\n•	Optimizing Electrical Systems: Improving the efficiency of motors, drives, and power distribution.\r\n•	Optimizing Mechanical Systems: Enhancing the performance of pumps, compressors, and mechanical drives.\r\n•	Workshop: Conducting an energy audit and developing an optimization plan.\r\nDay 9: Safety and Compliance in Integrated Maintenance\r\n•	Regulatory Compliance: Ensuring maintenance practices meet industry standards and regulations.\r\n•	Risk Management: Identifying and mitigating risks in electrical and mechanical maintenance.\r\n•	Emergency Preparedness: Planning for and responding to system failures and emergencies.\r\n•	Workshop: Developing a safety and compliance plan for an integrated maintenance program.\r\nDay 10: Final Project and Review\r\n•	Recap of Key Concepts: Review of major topics covered during the course.\r\n•	Capstone Project: Participants develop a comprehensive maintenance strategy for an integrated electrical and mechanical system.\r\n•	Project Presentation: Participants present their maintenance strategy, followed by feedback and discussion.\r\n•	Final Review and Q&A: Open discussion, course evaluation, and awarding of certificates.'),
(2695, '25-02-109-E2695', 44, 109, 256, 3, 10, '10', 1850.00, '2025-09-06', 1, NULL, 'The \"Mechanical Maintenance Strategies and Objectives\" training program is designed to provide participants with comprehensive knowledge and practical skills necessary for developing and implementing effective maintenance strategies. This two-week intensive course covers various aspects of mechanical maintenance, focusing on maximizing equipment efficiency, minimizing downtime, and achieving organizational objectives through strategic maintenance planning.', '•	Understand the fundamental principles of mechanical maintenance and its importance in industrial operations.\r\n•	Develop and implement effective maintenance strategies tailored to specific organizational needs.\r\n•	Learn how to optimize maintenance processes to improve equipment reliability and extend lifespan.\r\n•	Gain skills in using modern tools and techniques for predictive and preventive maintenance.\r\n•	Enhance problem-solving and decision-making abilities in maintenance-related scenarios.\r\n•	Align maintenance objectives with overall business goals to improve operational efficiency.', '•	Maintenance managers and engineers responsible for overseeing mechanical maintenance operations.\r\n•	Mechanical technicians and engineers involved in the maintenance and repair of industrial equipment.\r\n•	Professionals in charge of developing and implementing maintenance strategies within their organizations.\r\n•	Individuals seeking to enhance their skills in mechanical maintenance and asset management.', 'Week 1: Fundamentals and Strategy Development\r\nDay 1: Introduction to Mechanical Maintenance\r\n•	Welcome and Program Overview: Introduction to course objectives and structure.\r\n•	Importance of Mechanical Maintenance: Role in ensuring operational efficiency and longevity of equipment.\r\n•	Types of Maintenance: Preventive, Predictive, Corrective, and Condition-Based Maintenance.\r\n•	Case Study: Successful mechanical maintenance strategies in various industries.\r\nDay 2: Developing Maintenance Strategies\r\n•	Maintenance Strategy Fundamentals: Aligning strategies with organizational goals.\r\n•	Identifying Critical Equipment: Prioritizing assets based on operational importance.\r\n•	Developing a Maintenance Plan: Creating a structured approach to equipment maintenance.\r\n•	Workshop: Participants develop a preliminary maintenance strategy for their organization.\r\nDay 3: Preventive and Predictive Maintenance\r\n•	Principles of Preventive Maintenance: Scheduled inspections, servicing, and component replacement.\r\n•	Introduction to Predictive Maintenance: Using data and technology to anticipate failures.\r\n•	Tools and Techniques: Vibration analysis, thermography, and oil analysis.\r\n•	Workshop: Implementing preventive and predictive maintenance practices in a real-world scenario.\r\nDay 4: Maintenance Planning and Scheduling\r\n•	Maintenance Planning: Setting objectives, timelines, and resource allocation.\r\n•	Scheduling Maintenance Activities: Balancing maintenance needs with production demands.\r\n•	Computerized Maintenance Management Systems (CMMS): Role in planning and scheduling.\r\n•	Case Study: Effective maintenance planning and scheduling in a high-demand environment.\r\nDay 5: Risk Management in Maintenance\r\n•	Identifying Maintenance Risks: Understanding the impact of equipment failure.\r\n•	Risk Mitigation Strategies: Developing plans to reduce maintenance-related risks.\r\n•	Contingency Planning: Preparing for unexpected equipment failures.\r\n•	Workshop: Creating a risk management plan for mechanical maintenance activities.\r\nWeek 2: Advanced Strategies and Implementation\r\nDay 6: Reliability-Centered Maintenance (RCM)\r\n•	Introduction to RCM: Framework for determining optimal maintenance strategies.\r\n•	Failure Modes and Effects Analysis (FMEA): Identifying potential failure points.\r\n•	Implementing RCM: Steps for integrating RCM into existing maintenance practices.\r\n•	Workshop: Conducting an FMEA for key equipment in a simulated environment.\r\nDay 7: Total Productive Maintenance (TPM)\r\n•	Overview of TPM: Integrating maintenance into daily operations to improve overall efficiency.\r\n•	TPM Pillars: Autonomous maintenance, focused improvement, and more.\r\n•	Implementing TPM: Building a culture of continuous improvement and employee involvement.\r\n•	Case Study: Success stories of TPM implementation in manufacturing plants.\r\nDay 8: Asset Management and Lifecycle Costing\r\n•	Asset Management Principles: Strategies for managing the lifecycle of equipment.\r\n•	Lifecycle Costing: Evaluating the total cost of ownership and its impact on maintenance decisions.\r\n•	Optimizing Asset Utilization: Techniques for maximizing equipment value and performance.\r\n•	Workshop: Developing a lifecycle management plan for critical assets.\r\nDay 9: Continuous Improvement in Maintenance\r\n•	Importance of Continuous Improvement: Adapting to changes and improving processes over time.\r\n•	Maintenance Performance Metrics: Key indicators for measuring success.\r\n•	Lean Maintenance: Applying Lean principles to eliminate waste and enhance efficiency.\r\n•	Workshop: Implementing a continuous improvement plan in a maintenance department.\r\nDay 10: Final Project and Review\r\n•	Recap of Key Concepts: Review of major topics covered during the course.\r\n•	Capstone Project: Participants develop a comprehensive maintenance strategy for their organization.\r\n•	Project Presentation: Participants present their maintenance strategy, followed by feedback and discussion.\r\n•	Final Review and Q&A: Open discussion, course evaluation, and awarding of certificates.'),
(2696, '25-02-109-O2696', 44, 109, 7, 9, 10, '10', 2000.00, '2025-09-06', 1, NULL, 'This comprehensive training course concentrates on oil & gas production and subsequent treatment surface facility operations and processes. The course covers the entire oil & gas value chain from discovery, exploration and transportation through to refining and finally sales to downstream industries. An overview and fundamental understanding of the wide range of oilfield production handling and treatment equipment will be presented as well as new technologies.\r\nThis course emphasizes oil and gas production facilities both onshore and offshore - from the wellhead to delivery of an on-specification crude oil to the refinery or natural gas to downstream users. Produced water treating facilities are introduced and discussed and includes coverage of water injection systems. In addition to the engineering aspects of oil production facilities, practical operating problems will also be covered, including treatment of emulsions, handling of sand, dealing with wax and asphaltenes, natural gas dehydration and sweetening and refinery processing.', 'In this training course you will:\r\n•	Learn the origins and chemical characteristics of Oil & Gas\r\n•	Understand the challenges associated with offshore operations, horizontal drilling and other safety concerns\r\n•	Familiarize the participants with various methods and techniques used to explore, drill, produce, treat and transport Oil, Gas and their products\r\n•	Introduce the participants to upstream, mid-stream and downstream operations\r\n•	Gaining a deep understanding of the production of oil and gas \r\n•	Gaining knowledge of the different equipment and facilities found in oil and natural gas processing plants\r\n•	Appreciate Oil & Natural Gas treatment technologies and processes including produced water treatment\r\n•	Gain valuable knowledge of Gas Sweetening and Physical Solvents Unit Troubleshooting\r\n•	Gaining a deep understanding of the production of oil and gas \r\n•	Gaining knowledge of the different equipment and facilities found in oil and natural gas processing plants', '•	Petroleum, processing, operations, production engineers\r\n•	Chemical, Mechanical engineers and Process Technologists,\r\n•	Facility and Inspection engineers\r\n•	Maintenance or project engineers\r\n•	Technicians, support engineers and engineering trainees\r\n•	Consultants and Sales professionals', 'Module 1: Introduction of Oil & Gas Production Life Cycle\r\n\r\n•	The nature and formation of fossil fuels and oil reserves\r\n•	Basic petroleum geology- Where we find fossil fuels: Onshore/ offshore\r\n•	Type of wells and Reservoir types\r\n•	Exploration activities:\r\no	Exploration well\r\no	Directional Drilling\r\no	Drilling Offshore\r\no	Well Testing & Completion\r\no	Well Stimulation & Workover\r\n\r\n Module 2: Functions Related to Oil & Gas Operations\r\n\r\n•	The chemistry of fossil fuels and petroleum\r\n•	Physical properties of hydrocarbons\r\n•	Oil & Gas Gathering and separation\r\n•	On-site surface processing,\r\n•	Produced Water Generation and Management Practices\r\n•	Dew Point Control/Liquids Recovery\r\n•	Acid gas injection\r\n•	Oil Recovery Methods\r\n \r\nModule 3: Oil & Gas separation \r\n\r\n•	introduction to Oil & Gas Gravity Separation \r\n•	Why Separation\r\n•	Separation Conditions\r\n•	The Main Factors in Gravity Separation\r\n•	2 Phase & 3 Phase Separators\r\n•	Vertical & Horizontal Separators\r\n•	Separators Construction\r\n•	Application Of Internal Devices Used In Separators\r\n•	Production & Test Separators \r\n•	Special Types of Separators \r\n•	Vertical Separator Capacity\r\n•	Horizontal Separator Capacity\r\n•	Separator Pressure & Level Control\r\n•	Separator Start-Up Procedures \r\n•	Separator Shutdown Procedure\r\n•	Routine Operating Checks\r\n•	Separator Operation Exercise\r\n•	Isolation Types & Factors\r\n•	Operating Sequence to Prepare Vessel for Entry \r\n•	Operating Sequence to Prepare Vessel for Start-Up\r\n•	Separators Troubleshooting\r\n\r\nModule 4: Oil Desalting and Hydrogen Sulfide Stripping Technology\r\n\r\n•	Oil desalting \r\n•	Oil degassing & oil stabilization \r\n•	Oil H2S stripping \r\n\r\nModule 5: Oil Shipping and Storage\r\n\r\n•	Oil movement and tank farm duty.\r\n•	Crude oil receiving/storage\r\n•	Settling time & water draining\r\n•	Tank mixing & tank-tank transfer\r\n•	Product specifications & blending\r\n•	Example of overall monthly refinery balance\r\n•	Calculations for crude oil received\r\n•	Crude oil storage\r\n•	Types of storage tanks and functions\r\n•	Maintenance of storage tanks\r\n•	Tank maintenance\r\n•	Hazardous conditions and safety procedures \r\n•	Tank cleaning and desludging\r\n•	Permit to work system \r\n•	Storage tank measurements\r\n•	Types of errors\r\n•	Fundamentals of storage tanks calibrations\r\n•	Manual level gauging of storage tanks\r\n•	Automatic level gauging of storage tanks\r\n•	Free water measurements in storage tanks\r\n•	Temperature measurements in storage tanks\r\n•	Manual sampling of storage tanks\r\n•	Automatic sampling of storage tanks\r\n•	Advanced techniques in automatic storage tanks measurements \r\n•	Introduction to metering and meter proving\r\n•	Static measurement – storage tanks\r\n•	Dynamic measurement\r\n•	Meter factor& K factor\r\n•	Correction to meter reading\r\n•	Prover tank correction\r\n•	Pressure and temperature corrections\r\n•	Master meters\r\n•	Factors affecting the choice of meter proving equipment\r\n•	Displacement meter hardware\r\n•	Mechanical Automatic Temperature Compensation (ATC)\r\n•	Significance of Common Errors in Storage Tank Measurements\r\n\r\nModule 6: Special topics related to oil production and flow assurance\r\n\r\n•	Fluid property data and phase determination\r\n•	Fluid flow analysis – single and multiphase\r\n•	Slug formation and prediction\r\n•	Surge analysis\r\n•	Corrosion – internal and external\r\n•	Hydrate formation and prevention\r\n•	Wax formation and deposition, including prevention and remediation\r\n•	Asphaltene prediction, scale precipitation and sand production\r\n•	Pigging operations\r\n•	Leak detection methods and SCADA\r\n•	Startup/shutdown issues\r\n•	Pipeline optimization\r\n•	Process of optimizing of a pipeline\r\n•	Economic analysis, Life-cycle analysis and optimization of pipeline systems\r\n•	Network analysis\r\n•	Structural analysis of pipes\r\n•	Dynamic analyses\r\n•	Pipeline component design\r\n\r\nModule 7: Gas production and processing technology \r\n\r\n•	General Considerations and Safety\r\n•	Types of Contaminants\r\n•	Gas specifications\r\n•	Heating Value\r\n•	Sulphur Content\r\n•	Water Content\r\n•	Commercial Gases\r\n•	Ethane. Propane LPG\r\n•	Chemistry of Amine Gas Sweetening\r\n•	Process Selection and Classification\r\n•	Operating Problems\r\n•	Selective Sweetening Systems\r\n•	Process Flow and General Design Criteria / Guidelines\r\n•	MEA, DEA, MDEA, DGA, DIPA, and FLEXSORB\r\n•	Formulated Solvents\r\n•	Sterically Hindered Amines\r\n•	Design Procedures for Amine Systems\r\n•	Amine absorber\r\n•	Amine circulation rates\r\n•	Flash drum\r\n•	Amine re-boiler\r\n•	Amine stripper\r\n•	Overhead condenser and reflux accumulator\r\n•	Rich/lean amine exchanger\r\n•	Amine cooler\r\n•	Amine pumps &storage tanks \r\n•	Materials of construction\r\n•	Amine process variables &monitoring \r\n•	Amine plant startup &shutdown \r\n•	Amine plant maintenance \r\n•	Amine plant optimization \r\n•	General Operating Problems in Amine Processes\r\n•	General Considerations for Amine Processes\r\n•	Claus Process Considerations and Modifications\r\n•	Typical PFD – 3 Stage\r\n•	Process Considerations\r\n•	Mechanical Considerations\r\n•	Instrumentation\r\n•	Tail Gas Handling\r\n•	Incineration\r\n•	Clean-up\r\n•	SO2 Recovery Process\r\n•	H2S Recovery Process\r\n•	Direct Oxidation Process\r\n•	Liquid Redox\r\n•	The EUROCLAUS Concept\r\n•	SCOT\r\n•	Claus Process Calculations and Exercises\r\n•	Sulfur Product Specifications\r\n•	Sulfur Storage and Handling\r\n•	Safety and Environmental Considerations\r\n•	Sulfur Recovery plant startup &shutdown and maintenance  \r\n•	Troubleshooting: what can go wrong\r\n•	Course review and evaluation\r\n•	Plant optimization \r\n\r\nModule 8: Production water treatment\r\n\r\n•	Water content of natural gas\r\n•	Hydrate formation, Hazard, prevention and handling \r\n•	Types of dehydration processes: absorption, adsorption and condensation\r\n•	Dew point & Dew point depression\r\n•	Glycol dehydration size \r\n•	Mass transfer operations: absorption and stripping, trays vs. packing\r\n•	TEG equipment: gas scrubbers, glycol contactors, flash tank, filters, lean/rich heat exchanger, regenerator, stripping gas\r\n•	Operating procedures and problems for TEG systems: startup and shutdown, normal operations, glycol losses, corrosion, troubleshooting\r\n•	Care of the TEG solution\r\n•	Mole sieve gas dehydration\r\n•	Absorbers and desiccants\r\n•	Mass transfer zone\r\n•	Regeneration system\r\n•	Operation and adsorbent life\r\n•	Mole Sieve operating problems and troubleshooting\r\n•	Mole Sieve loading &unloading procedures \r\n•	Refrigeration circuit basic design /operation\r\n•	Capacity control \r\n•	Refrigeration equipment: surge drum, expansion valve, chiller, compressor and condenser  \r\n•	Operation troubleshooting and optimization \r\n•	Fractionation process principle\r\n•	Fractionation tower design trays & packaging \r\n•	Fractionation equipment: preheater. tower. reflux condenser, reflux pump and re-boiler.\r\n•	Fractionation process variable and control system \r\n•	Fractionation process troubleshooting and optimization \r\n•	Minimization of pipeline running cost\r\n•	Planning of cost efficient operations\r\n\r\nModule 9: Equipment used in oil and gas production\r\n\r\n•	Pumps \r\n•	Compressors \r\n•	Heat exchangers \r\n•	Piping and valves \r\n•	Heaters \r\n•	Control system\r\n\r\nModule 10: Safety management in oil and gas production\r\n\r\n•	Process safety management \r\n•	Accident investigation \r\n•	HAZOP'),
(2697, '25-02-101-M2697', 51, 101, 361, 1, 1, '65', 5275.00, '2025-09-30', 1, NULL, 'This 13-week Project Management training course is designed to equip participants with the essential skills and methodologies required to plan, execute, monitor, and successfully close projects. The program provides a balance between theory and practice, introducing industry-recognized frameworks, tools, and techniques that help professionals achieve project goals within scope, time, and budget constraints.', '1.	Understand fundamental concepts and principles of project management.\r\n2.	Learn how to initiate, plan, execute, monitor, and close projects.\r\n3.	Apply practical tools for project scheduling, budgeting, and risk management.\r\n4.	Develop leadership, communication, and teamwork skills in project settings.\r\n5.	Prepare participants for professional growth and certification readiness.', '1.	Project coordinators, team leaders, and supervisors.\r\n2.	Professionals seeking to transition into project management roles.\r\n3.	Engineers, IT specialists, and business staff involved in projects.\r\n4.	Students and graduates aspiring to gain project management skills.', 'Week 1 – Introduction to Project Management\r\n•	What is a project? Project vs. operations\r\n•	Importance of project management in organizations\r\n•	Project life cycle and process groups\r\n•	Roles and responsibilities of project managers\r\nWeek 2 – Project Environment & Stakeholders\r\n•	Project governance and organizational structures\r\n•	Identifying stakeholders\r\n•	Stakeholder analysis and engagement strategies\r\nWeek 3 – Project Initiation\r\n•	Developing project charter\r\n•	Defining project objectives and success criteria\r\n•	Business case and feasibility studies\r\nWeek 4 – Project Planning (Scope & Work Breakdown Structures)\r\n•	Defining project scope and requirements\r\n•	Creating a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)\r\n•	Scope validation and control\r\nWeek 5 – Project Planning (Schedule & Budget)\r\n•	Developing project schedules (Gantt charts, network diagrams)\r\n•	Estimating costs and preparing budgets\r\n•	Resource planning\r\nWeek 6 – Risk & Quality Planning\r\n•	Identifying project risks\r\n•	Qualitative and quantitative risk analysis\r\n•	Risk response planning\r\n•	Quality standards and control measures\r\nWeek 7 – Project Execution (Team Building)\r\n•	Leadership styles in project management\r\n•	Motivating teams and managing conflicts\r\n•	Delegation and accountability\r\nWeek 8 – Project Execution (Communication)\r\n•	Communication planning and methods\r\n•	Effective reporting and project meetings\r\n•	Using collaboration tools\r\nWeek 9 – Monitoring & Controlling Projects\r\n•	Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)\r\n•	Project dashboards and progress tracking\r\n•	Change management and control processes\r\nWeek 10 – Project Tools and Technology\r\n•	MS Project basics\r\n•	Agile tools (Trello, Jira)\r\n•	Excel for project management\r\nWeek 11 – Case Studies and Real-world Scenarios\r\n•	Review of successful and failed projects\r\n•	Lessons learned from industry examples\r\n•	Group discussions\r\nWeek 12 – Project Closing\r\n•	Closing activities and documentation\r\n•	Post-project evaluation\r\n•	Knowledge transfer and lessons learned\r\nWeek 13 – Final Review & Assessment\r\n•	Group project presentations\r\n•	Comprehensive course review\r\n•	Written/Practical assessment'),
(2698, '25-02-101-M2698', 51, 101, 360, 1, 2, '130', 6935.00, '2025-09-30', 1, NULL, 'This 26-week comprehensive Project Management training program offers in-depth knowledge and advanced techniques for managing projects across industries. It expands beyond the essentials to cover strategic alignment, advanced methodologies, and professional practices. Participants gain exposure to real-world case studies, simulations, and tools that build the competencies required for leadership in project-oriented environments.', '1.	Master the full project management body of knowledge, from basics to advanced practices.\r\n2.	Apply diverse methodologies, including Agile, Waterfall, and hybrid approaches.\r\n3.	Build advanced competencies in risk management, procurement, and stakeholder engagement.\r\n4.	Strengthen strategic thinking, decision-making, and problem-solving abilities.\r\n5.	Enhance readiness for global project management certifications (PMP®, PRINCE2®, Agile, etc.).', '1.	Mid- to senior-level professionals managing large or complex projects.\r\n2.	Organizations seeking to build internal project management capabilities.\r\n3.	Individuals preparing for advanced certifications in project management.\r\n4.	Anyone aiming to establish a career as a professional project manager.', 'Week 1 – Introduction to Project Management\r\n•	Definition, importance, and applications of project management\r\n•	Project life cycle and process groups\r\nWeek 2 – Project Manager’s Role & Organizational Context\r\n•	Competencies of effective project managers\r\n•	Project governance and organizational structures\r\nWeek 3 – Stakeholders and Strategic Alignment\r\n•	Stakeholder analysis and engagement\r\n•	Linking projects to organizational strategy\r\nWeek 4 – Project Initiation & Business Case Development\r\n•	Project charters, feasibility studies, and success criteria\r\nWeek 5 – Scope Management (Part 1)\r\n•	Collecting requirements and defining scope\r\n•	Work Breakdown Structures\r\nWeek 6 – Scope Management (Part 2)\r\n•	Scope verification and control techniques\r\nWeek 7 – Time Management (Part 1)\r\n•	Scheduling techniques: Gantt, CPM, PERT\r\nWeek 8 – Time Management (Part 2)\r\n•	Critical path method and resource leveling\r\nWeek 9 – Cost Management (Part 1)\r\n•	Estimating techniques (analogous, bottom-up, parametric)\r\nWeek 10 – Cost Management (Part 2)\r\n•	Budgeting and cost baseline preparation\r\nWeek 11 – Risk Management (Part 1)\r\n•	Identifying and categorizing risks\r\nWeek 12 – Risk Management (Part 2)\r\n•	Qualitative and quantitative risk analysis\r\nWeek 13 – Risk Management (Part 3)\r\n•	Risk response planning and monitoring\r\nWeek 14 – Quality Management\r\n•	Quality planning, assurance, and control tools\r\nWeek 15 – Procurement Management\r\n•	Contract types, vendor selection, procurement processes\r\nWeek 16 – Project Execution (Leadership)\r\n•	Leading teams, managing conflicts, motivation techniques\r\nWeek 17 – Project Execution (Communication)\r\n•	Developing communication plans and managing information flow\r\nWeek 18 – Monitoring & Controlling (Part 1)\r\n•	Earned Value Management (EVM) basics\r\nWeek 19 – Monitoring & Controlling (Part 2)\r\n•	Advanced reporting, dashboards, and project audits\r\nWeek 20 – Project Management Information Systems\r\n•	MS Project, Primavera, Agile boards (Jira, Trello)\r\nWeek 21 – Agile Project Management (Part 1)\r\n•	Agile principles and values\r\n•	Scrum framework basics\r\nWeek 22 – Agile Project Management (Part 2)\r\n•	Hybrid project management approaches\r\nWeek 23 – Change & Stakeholder Management\r\n•	Change control processes\r\n•	Advanced stakeholder engagement\r\nWeek 24 – Ethics, Governance & Legal Aspects\r\n•	Professional ethics in project management\r\n•	Governance models and compliance\r\nWeek 25 – Capstone Simulation\r\n•	Group exercise: manage a full project scenario (initiation → closure)\r\nWeek 26 – Final Presentations & Certification Preparation\r\n•	Group project presentations\r\n•	PMP®, PRINCE2®, Agile exam preparation overview\r\n•	Course wrap-up and evaluations'),
(2699, '25-02-115-C2699', 52, 115, 362, 5, 5, '10', 1950.00, '2025-10-12', 1, NULL, 'Understanding Artificial Intelligence: Empowering the Blind Community through AI Awareness and Practical Knowledge', 'To equip blind participants with a strong understanding of artificial intelligence concepts, real-world applications, and accessible AI tools that can enhance independence, productivity, and social inclusion.\r\n\r\nBy the end of the course, participants will be able to:\r\nUnderstand what AI is and how it works conceptually.\r\nIdentify real-world AI applications in daily life.\r\nUse voice assistants and screen-reader–friendly AI tools effectively.\r\nExplore AI accessibility technologies (text-to-speech, OCR, image recognition, etc.).\r\nUnderstand AI’s impact on jobs, education, and society.\r\nRecognize the ethics and limitations of AI.\r\nGain confidence in adopting AI tools for personal and professional use.', 'Blind and visually impaired adults and students interested in learning how AI affects their daily lives and how they can benefit from it — without the need to code or program.', 'Day 1: Introduction to Artificial Intelligence\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\nWhat is AI? Everyday examples.\r\nAI vs. human intelligence.\r\nHistory of AI in simple terms.\r\n\r\nActivity: Audio quiz on identifying AI around us (e.g., Siri, Google Assistant, ChatGPT).\r\n\r\nTools: Voice-based slides, tactile diagrams (optional).\r\n\r\nDay 2: How AI Thinks – Basic Concepts\r\nTopics:\r\nData, learning, and decision-making.\r\nHow machines “learn” through examples.\r\nSimple analogy: teaching a child vs. teaching an AI.\r\n\r\nActivity: Group storytelling — participants simulate “training” an AI verbally.\r\n\r\nDay 3: AI in Everyday Life\r\nTopics:\r\nSmart home assistants.\r\nAI in transportation, health, and entertainment.\r\nAI for accessibility (voice commands, speech recognition, audio description).\r\nActivity: Guided demo using accessible AI apps (e.g., Seeing AI, Be My Eyes, ChatGPT Voice).\r\n\r\nDay 4: AI for Accessibility\r\nTopics:\r\nText-to-speech (TTS), speech-to-text (STT).\r\nImage recognition and object detection apps.\r\nOCR (Optical Character Recognition) and real-time translators.\r\n\r\nActivity: Hands-on practice using AI accessibility apps on mobile.\r\n\r\nDay 5: Communication and Productivity with AI\r\nTopics:\r\nUsing AI for writing, summarizing, scheduling, and email.\r\nUsing ChatGPT and voice AI for assistance.\r\n\r\nActivity: Workshop — “Ask AI to help with your daily task”.\r\n\r\nDay 6: AI in Education and Learning\r\nTopics:\r\nHow AI supports studying (note-taking, research, summaries).\r\nAI-based language learning and tutoring.\r\nActivity: Try AI tutoring via voice assistants (e.g., Ask ChatGPT about a topic).\r\n\r\nDay 7: AI in Work and Employment\r\nTopics:\r\nHow AI is changing jobs.\r\nNew opportunities for blind professionals using AI tools.\r\n\r\nActivity: Case studies — success stories of visually impaired people using AI at work.\r\n\r\nDay 8: Ethics and Risks of AI\r\nTopics:\r\nPrivacy, bias, misinformation, and AI dependence.\r\nResponsible use of AI tools.\r\n\r\nActivity: Discussion: “Can AI replace human judgment?”\r\n\r\nDay 9: Future of AI and Inclusion\r\nTopics:\r\nAI trends that empower persons with disabilities.\r\nThe future of accessible design.\r\n\r\nActivity: Brainstorm — “What would you want AI to do for you in the future?”\r\n\r\nDay 10: Review, Reflection, and Certification\r\nTopics:\r\nRecap of major lessons.\r\nSharing experiences and personal takeaways.\r\nEvaluation through oral questions or group reflection.\r\n\r\nActivity: “My AI Story” – each participant shares how they plan to use AI.'),
(2700, '25-02-101-M2700', 51, 101, 363, 1, 1, '130', 11785.00, '2025-10-28', 1, NULL, 'In the highly competitive and safety-sensitive oil and gas industry, the Human Resources (HR) function plays a crucial role in attracting, developing, and retaining top talent while ensuring compliance with complex labor, environmental, and safety regulations.\r\nThis Human Resources program is designed to strengthen HR professionals’ capabilities in strategic workforce management, competency development, industrial relations, and performance optimization — aligned with the unique operational and safety challenges of the oil and gas sector.', 'By the end of the program, participants will be able to:\r\n1.	Understand the strategic role of HR in supporting oil and gas operations.\r\n2.	Apply modern HR planning and talent management strategies.\r\n3.	Implement effective recruitment, selection, and onboarding processes for technical and non-technical staff.\r\n4.	Manage performance and develop competency frameworks aligned with operational needs.\r\n5.	Ensure compliance with local labor laws, HSE (Health, Safety, and Environment) requirements, and corporate policies.\r\n6.	Strengthen employee engagement, retention, and succession planning initiatives.\r\n7.	Build HR digital capabilities and analytical skills for data-driven decision-making.', '•	HR Managers and Officers\r\n•	Recruitment Specialists\r\n•	Training and Development Officers\r\n•	Compensation and Benefits Analysts\r\n•	Employee Relations Staff\r\n•	Supervisors transitioning into HR roles', 'Module 1: Foundations of HR in the Oil & Gas Industry (Weeks 1–3)\r\n•	Overview of the Oil and Gas Sector and HR Challenges\r\n•	Organizational Structures and Job Classifications\r\n•	Role of HR in Safety Culture and Compliance\r\n•	HR as a Strategic Business Partner\r\n\r\nModule 2: Workforce Planning and Talent Acquisition (Weeks 4–7)\r\n•	Strategic Workforce Planning\r\n•	Recruitment and Selection for Technical Roles\r\n•	Competency-Based Interviewing Techniques\r\n•	Employer Branding in the Energy Sector\r\n•	Onboarding and Integration Best Practices\r\n\r\nModule 3: Learning, Development, and Competency Management (Weeks 8–11)\r\n•	Training Needs Analysis\r\n•	Competency Frameworks for Oil & Gas Jobs\r\n•	Career Pathing and Succession Planning\r\n•	Evaluating Training ROI and Effectiveness\r\n•	Implementing Learning Management Systems (LMS)\r\n\r\nModule 4: Performance Management and Employee Engagement (Weeks 12–15)\r\n•	Designing Effective Performance Appraisal Systems\r\n•	Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) for Technical Staff\r\n•	Coaching, Feedback, and Continuous Improvement\r\n•	Employee Motivation and Retention Strategies\r\n\r\nModule 5: Compensation, Benefits, and Industrial Relations (Weeks 16–19)\r\n•	Compensation Structures and Pay Scales\r\n•	Shift Schedules, Overtime, and Hazard Allowances\r\n•	Collective Bargaining and Union Relations\r\n•	Managing Grievances and Disciplinary Procedures\r\n•	Legal and Ethical HR Practices\r\n\r\nModule 6: HR Data Analytics and Digital Transformation (Weeks 20–23)\r\n•	HR Information Systems (HRIS) in Oil & Gas\r\n•	Data Analytics for HR Decision-Making\r\n•	HR Dashboards and KPIs\r\n•	Digital HR Trends and Automation Tools\r\n\r\nModule 7: Capstone Project and Presentation (Weeks 24–26)\r\n•	Applied HR Improvement Project in Oil & Gas Context\r\n•	Group Work: Propose a Strategic HR Initiative\r\n•	Presentation to Panel of Industry Experts\r\n•	Evaluation and Certification'),
(2701, '25-02-103-M2701', 59, 103, 366, 1, 50, '5', 1100.00, '2025-11-06', 1, NULL, 'In today’s competitive and rapidly changing business environment, effective management is vital to ensure productivity, motivation, and sustainable performance. This Managerial Skills Development course is designed to strengthen the essential competencies required by managers, supervisors, and team leaders to lead effectively, communicate clearly, and make informed decisions.\r\nThe program integrates practical exercises, group discussions, and case studies to develop both the behavioral and technical aspects of management, helping participants translate concepts into workplace performance.', 'By the end of this course, participants will be able to:\r\n1.	Understand the key roles and responsibilities of modern managers.\r\n2.	Enhance communication, motivation, and delegation skills.\r\n3.	Apply effective planning, organizing, and problem-solving techniques.\r\n4.	Manage performance and handle difficult situations constructively.\r\n5.	Strengthen leadership and decision-making abilities to achieve team goals.', 'This course is suitable for:\r\n•	Current and newly appointed managers, supervisors, and team leaders.\r\n•	Project coordinators and department heads responsible for people management.\r\n•	Individuals being prepared for promotional leadership roles.', 'Day 1: Understanding the Role of a Manager\r\n•	Transition from employee to manager\r\n•	Key managerial functions and competencies\r\n•	Balancing leadership, management, and administration\r\n•	Time and priority management\r\nDay 2: Effective Communication and Interpersonal Skills\r\n•	Principles of effective workplace communication\r\n•	Active listening and constructive feedback\r\n•	Building trust and rapport within the team\r\n•	Managing meetings and difficult conversations\r\nDay 3: Motivation and Team Leadership\r\n•	Theories and techniques of motivation\r\n•	Empowering and engaging employees\r\n•	Leadership styles and situational leadership\r\n•	Developing high-performing teams\r\nDay 4: Problem Solving and Decision Making\r\n•	Analytical and creative problem-solving models\r\n•	Decision-making tools and techniques\r\n•	Managing conflict and negotiation skills\r\n•	Risk management and accountability in decisions\r\nDay 5: Performance Management and Continuous Improvement\r\n•	Setting SMART objectives and KPIs\r\n•	Coaching and mentoring for performance improvement\r\n•	Handling underperformance\r\n•	Building a culture of continuous learning and innovation\r\n•	Final group exercise and action plan presentation\r\n\r\nConclusion\r\nThis Managerial Skills Development program is a comprehensive initiative aimed at empowering managers and supervisors with practical tools, strategies, and insights to lead their teams effectively and achieve organizational goals.\r\nWe are committed to delivering impactful learning experiences that produce measurable improvements in managerial performance and productivity.'),
(2702, '25-02-103-E2702', 59, 103, 365, 3, 100, '5', 1100.00, '2025-11-06', 1, NULL, 'Efficient maintenance operations management is vital for ensuring the reliability, safety, and performance of industrial assets. This 5-day intensive program equips participants with modern principles, tools, and strategies to manage maintenance activities effectively. It blends technical knowledge with management techniques to improve asset performance, reduce downtime, and optimize maintenance costs.\r\nThe training emphasizes best practices in preventive, predictive, and reliability-centered maintenance (RCM), and introduces digital tools for maintenance planning, work order management, and performance monitoring.\r\nExpected Outcomes\r\nAfter completing this program, participants will gain the skills to:\r\n•	Design and implement a structured maintenance management system.\r\n•	Improve reliability and reduce equipment downtime.\r\n•	Enhance maintenance cost efficiency and performance.\r\n•	Use CMMS tools effectively for maintenance tracking and reporting.\r\n•	Develop a continuous improvement plan for their maintenance operations.', 'Upon completion of this training, participants will be able to:\r\n•	Understand the role and objectives of maintenance operations in asset management.\r\n•	Plan, organize, and control maintenance activities to improve equipment reliability.\r\n•	Apply preventive and predictive maintenance strategies.\r\n•	Implement maintenance performance indicators (KPIs) and benchmarking.\r\n•	Optimize spare parts, manpower, and maintenance resources.\r\n•	Develop and implement effective maintenance schedules and plans.\r\n•	Utilize computerized maintenance management systems (CMMS).\r\n•	Apply safety, risk, and quality standards in maintenance operations.', 'This course is designed for:\r\n•	Maintenance Engineers and Supervisors\r\n•	Operations Managers\r\n•	Plant Engineers\r\n•	Maintenance Planners and Schedulers\r\n•	Technical Superintendents\r\n•	Reliability Engineers\r\n•	Facility and Asset Managers\r\n•	Any professional involved in maintenance management decision-making', 'Day 1 – Fundamentals of Maintenance Operations\r\n•	Overview of maintenance management concepts\r\n•	Maintenance objectives and performance indicators\r\n•	Types of maintenance: Corrective, Preventive, Predictive, and Proactive\r\n•	Maintenance policies and organizational structures\r\n•	Maintenance cost analysis and budgeting principles\r\nDay 2 – Planning and Scheduling of Maintenance\r\n•	Maintenance planning process and workflow\r\n•	Work order system and documentation\r\n•	Scheduling principles and prioritization techniques\r\n•	Resource planning: manpower, materials, tools, and time\r\n•	Planning for shutdowns and turnarounds\r\nDay 3 – Maintenance Strategies and Reliability\r\n•	Reliability-centered maintenance (RCM) overview\r\n•	Failure modes and effects analysis (FMEA)\r\n•	Predictive maintenance technologies (vibration, thermography, oil analysis)\r\n•	Root cause analysis (RCA)\r\n•	Case studies: Improving reliability and availability\r\nDay 4 – Performance Management and Optimization\r\n•	Maintenance KPIs and benchmarking\r\n•	Measuring Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) and Mean Time To Repair (MTTR)\r\n•	Continuous improvement in maintenance\r\n•	Cost optimization and lifecycle asset management\r\n•	Integration of maintenance with production and safety systems\r\nDay 5 – Digitalization and Maintenance Management Systems\r\n•	Introduction to CMMS and ERP integration\r\n•	Data management and maintenance analytics\r\n•	Implementing digital maintenance workflows\r\n•	Safety, quality, and risk management in maintenance operations\r\n•	Workshop: Developing a maintenance improvement plan\r\n•	Final assessment and feedback\r\n\r\nConclusion\r\nThis 5-day program on Maintenance Operations Management provides a practical and strategic approach to modern maintenance practices. Participants will return with the knowledge and tools to enhance maintenance performance, reduce costs, and improve equipment reliability in their organizations.'),
(2703, '25-02-103-E2703', 59, 103, 367, 3, 150, '5', 1100.00, '2025-11-06', 1, NULL, 'The Gas Turbine Operation course provides participants with an in-depth understanding of the principles, design, and operational aspects of gas turbines used in power generation and industrial applications. The training emphasizes practical knowledge of turbine components, system operations, performance monitoring, troubleshooting, and maintenance strategies to ensure optimal performance and reliability.', 'Upon completion of this course, participants will be able to:\r\n•	Understand the fundamental principles of gas turbine operation and thermodynamics.\r\n•	Identify major components and their functions within the gas turbine system.\r\n•	Analyze gas turbine performance parameters and operating characteristics.\r\n•	Recognize common operational challenges and troubleshooting techniques.\r\n•	Apply best practices for startup, shutdown, and load control operations.\r\n•	Understand maintenance requirements and performance optimization techniques.', 'This course is designed for:\r\n•	Operations and maintenance engineers\r\n•	Power plant operators and technicians\r\n•	Mechanical and electrical engineers\r\n•	Supervisors and team leaders in energy production\r\n•	Professionals involved in the operation or management of gas turbine systems', 'Day 1 – Fundamentals of Gas Turbine Operation\r\n•	Introduction to gas turbines and their applications\r\n•	Basic thermodynamic cycles: Brayton cycle and energy conversion\r\n•	Types of gas turbines (aero-derivative and heavy-duty)\r\n•	Overview of turbine systems and processes\r\n\r\nDay 2 – Major Components and Systems\r\n•	Compressor design and operation\r\n•	Combustion chambers and fuel systems\r\n•	Turbine section and power generation principles\r\n•	Lubrication, cooling, and sealing systems\r\n\r\nDay 3 – Control, Instrumentation, and Operation\r\n•	Turbine control systems and automation\r\n•	Startup, loading, and shutdown procedures\r\n•	Monitoring and control of operational parameters\r\n•	Safety systems and emergency shutdown mechanisms\r\n\r\nDay 4 – Performance and Troubleshooting\r\n•	Efficiency and performance evaluation\r\n•	Common operational problems and fault diagnosis\r\n•	Vibration analysis and monitoring techniques\r\n•	Troubleshooting case studies\r\n\r\nDay 5 – Maintenance and Best Practices\r\n•	Preventive and predictive maintenance strategies\r\n•	Maintenance scheduling and documentation\r\n•	Operational optimization and reliability improvement\r\n•	Course review and open discussion / Q&A session\r\n\r\nConclusion\r\nThis course equips participants with the necessary knowledge and practical understanding to ensure safe, efficient, and reliable operation of gas turbines. It is ideal for companies aiming to enhance plant performance, improve operational reliability, and develop their technical workforce to international standards.');
INSERT INTO `quotations` (`quot_id`, `quot_ref`, `ord_id`, `cust_id`, `course_id`, `cat_id`, `trainees`, `duration`, `cost`, `quot_date`, `ven_id`, `file_path`, `introduction`, `objectives`, `audiences`, `outlines`) VALUES
(2704, '25-02-103-E2704', 59, 103, 364, 3, 107, '5', 1100.00, '2025-11-06', 1, NULL, 'The natural gas industry plays a vital role in global energy systems, supplying cleaner fuel for industrial, commercial, and domestic sectors. Efficient and safe transportation of natural gas from production fields to end users requires advanced technical and managerial expertise.\r\nThis 5-day intensive course on Natural Gas Transportation Operations Management provides comprehensive knowledge and practical insight into the design, operation, maintenance, and management of gas transmission and distribution systems. Participants will gain a clear understanding of pipeline networks, compressor station operations, safety procedures, and the regulatory framework governing gas transportation.\r\n\r\nExpected Outcomes\r\nUpon completion of this training, participants will:\r\n•	Acquire a holistic understanding of gas transportation operations.\r\n•	Be capable of managing day-to-day pipeline system operations effectively.\r\n•	Implement improved safety and integrity management systems.\r\n•	Enhance organizational performance through optimized operations management.', 'By the end of this training, participants will be able to:\r\n•	Understand the fundamentals of natural gas properties, flow, and behavior in pipelines.\r\n•	Explain the design principles and operational strategies for gas transmission networks.\r\n•	Manage and monitor transportation operations effectively to ensure reliability and safety.\r\n•	Apply international standards, codes, and best practices in gas transportation management.\r\n•	Identify, analyze, and mitigate risks associated with gas transportation systems.\r\n•	Integrate maintenance, inspection, and integrity management programs.\r\n•	Optimize operational performance and energy efficiency across transportation assets.', 'This course is designed for:\r\n•	Engineers, supervisors, and technical staff involved in gas transmission and distribution.\r\n•	Pipeline operations and maintenance personnel.\r\n•	Gas supply and dispatching engineers.\r\n•	HSE professionals in gas transportation operations.\r\n•	Managers and decision-makers responsible for pipeline and energy infrastructure.\r\n•	Professionals seeking to upgrade their technical and managerial competencies in gas logistics.', 'Day 1 – Fundamentals of Natural Gas & Transportation Systems\r\n•	Overview of the natural gas industry value chain.\r\n•	Composition, properties, and classification of natural gas.\r\n•	Introduction to gas transmission and distribution networks.\r\n•	Gas flow principles in pipelines (steady & transient flow).\r\n•	Components of a gas transportation system: pipelines, valves, meters, regulators, and compressors.\r\nDay 2 – Pipeline Design and Construction\r\n•	Pipeline design considerations and route selection.\r\n•	Material selection and pipeline sizing.\r\n•	Pressure, temperature, and flow calculations.\r\n•	Construction methods and quality assurance.\r\n•	Commissioning and pre-operation testing.\r\nDay 3 – Operations and Control of Gas Transportation\r\n•	Operation of compressor and metering stations.\r\n•	Gas dispatching and SCADA system overview.\r\n•	Gas balancing and line-pack management.\r\n•	Maintenance scheduling and operation optimization.\r\n•	Operational safety and emergency response.\r\nDay 4 – Integrity Management and Safety\r\n•	Pipeline inspection techniques (pigging, smart pigs, leak detection).\r\n•	Corrosion control and cathodic protection systems.\r\n•	Integrity assessment and defect management.\r\n•	Risk analysis and hazard identification (HAZOP, HAZID).\r\n•	Safety management systems and compliance with standards (API, ASME, ISO).\r\nDay 5 – Management and Regulatory Framework\r\n•	Gas transportation economics and tariff structures.\r\n•	Contracting and regulatory requirements.\r\n•	Environmental considerations and sustainability in gas transport.\r\n•	Case studies of pipeline operation incidents and lessons learned.\r\n•	Group workshop and final assessment.\r\n\r\nThis Natural Gas Transportation Operations Management program is designed to strengthen technical and managerial capabilities in the operation of gas transmission systems. Through a balance of theory and practical applications, the course empowers participants to enhance system reliability, ensure safety compliance, and improve operational efficiency.'),
(2705, '25-02-104-C2705', 57, 104, 369, 5, 4, '10', 1400.00, '2025-09-30', 1, NULL, 'Course Introduction', 'Objectives', 'Target Audience', 'Course Outlines'),
(2706, '25-02-103-E2706', 59, 103, 371, 3, 5, '5', 1100.00, '2025-11-30', 1, NULL, 'This one-week intensive course provides participants with a practical and comprehensive overview of maintenance management practices for power generation plants and electrical transmission networks. The program is designed to familiarize maintenance staff, supervisors, and engineers with best practices in preventive and corrective maintenance, system monitoring, safety standards, and reliability-centered maintenance (RCM) tailored for generation and transmission facilities.\r\nThrough a blend of theoretical sessions and practical exercises, the course addresses how to plan, schedule, and execute maintenance activities effectively to maximize system uptime, minimize downtime and failures, and ensure safe and efficient operations of generation and transmission assets.', 'By the end of this course, participants will be able to:\r\n1.	Understand the structure and components of power generation plants and transmission networks.\r\n2.	Recognize common failure modes and operational risks in generation and transmission systems.\r\n3.	Apply maintenance planning and scheduling techniques to ensure reliability and continuity.\r\n4.	Implement preventive and predictive maintenance strategies to reduce unplanned outages.\r\n5.	Use inspection, monitoring and diagnostic tools to assess asset health.\r\n6.	Ensure safety and compliance with technical and regulatory standards during maintenance activities.\r\n7.	Develop maintenance procedures and checklists adapted to their facility’s context.\r\n8.	Improve coordination between operations, maintenance, and safety teams to enhance system performance.\r\n\r\n\r\nAfter completing the course, participants will have acquired:\r\n•	Knowledge of power generation and transmission systems and their maintenance requirements.\r\n•	Ability to identify potential failure modes and plan appropriate maintenance interventions.\r\n•	Skills in preventive and predictive maintenance techniques, inspection, and diagnostic methods.\r\n•	Capability to develop and implement maintenance schedules, checklists, and documentation procedures.\r\n•	Understanding of safety and compliance requirements specific to power industry maintenance.\r\n•	Competence in coordination and collaboration between maintenance, operations, and safety teams.\r\n•	Awareness of performance monitoring and continuous improvement in asset management.', 'This course is intended for:\r\n•	Maintenance technicians and engineers in power generation plants.\r\n•	Supervisors and shift-leaders responsible for maintenance planning.\r\n•	Electrical network and transmission staff.\r\n•	Operations personnel involved in plant reliability and safety.\r\n•	Newly promoted maintenance coordinators seeking updated practices.\r\n•	Quality, safety, and reliability officers overseeing generation/transmission assets.', 'Day 1 — Overview of Power Generation & Transmission Systems\r\n•	Types of power plants (thermal, gas, hydro, etc.) and transmission network structure\r\n•	Major components: generators, turbines, transformers, switchgear, transmission lines, protection systems\r\n•	Overview of maintenance: reactive, preventive, predictive, and reliability-centered maintenance\r\nDay 2 — Risk Identification & Failure Modes in Generation & Transmission\r\n•	Common failure causes (mechanical, electrical, environmental, operational)\r\n•	Risk assessment methods for power assets\r\n•	Safety standards, regulatory compliance, and permit-to-work procedures\r\nDay 3 — Maintenance Planning & Scheduling Techniques\r\n•	Developing maintenance plans and schedules for generation and transmission assets\r\n•	Preventive vs predictive maintenance methods\r\n•	Use of condition monitoring, diagnostic tools (vibration analysis, thermal imaging, electrical testing)\r\nDay 4 — Asset Inspection & Diagnostic Tools / Preventive Maintenance\r\n•	Inspection techniques: visual inspection, electrical testing, thermal scans, load tests\r\n•	Maintenance checklists and documentation best practices\r\nDay 5 — Maintenance Management, Coordination & Continuous Improvement\r\n•	Maintenance management systems: documentation, reporting, KPIs (downtime rate, availability, MTBF/MTTR)\r\n•	Coordination between operation, maintenance, and safety teams\r\n•	Developing maintenance procedures, emergency response plans, and improvement proposals\r\n•	Final exercise: draft a maintenance program plan for a hypothetical power generation facility / substation'),
(2707, '25-02-103-C2707', 59, 103, 370, 5, 8, '5', 1100.00, '2025-11-30', 1, NULL, 'This intensive one-week training equips participants with modern skills and methodologies to analyze, organize, and enhance administrative procedures and workflows. The course combines theory and practical exercises in process mapping, workflow analysis, documentation of procedures, and redesign of administrative tasks — with the purpose of streamlining operations, reducing inefficiencies, and improving overall organizational performance. Participants learn to produce clear, efficient procedures, minimize redundancy, and foster continuous improvement within their administrative units.', 'By the end of the training, participants will be able to:\r\n1.	Understand the concept and importance of formalized administrative procedures for institutional efficiency.\r\n2.	Map and document existing workflows and procedures in their departments.\r\n3.	Analyze current procedures to identify bottlenecks, redundancies, and inefficiencies.\r\n4.	Design improved, simplified, and standardized procedures compatible with organizational goals.\r\n5.	Apply tools and modern methods (e.g., process flowcharts, SIPOC, Lean thinking) for process improvement.\r\n6.	Implement changes to administrative workflows, ensuring clarity, accountability, and compliance.\r\n7.	Contribute to building a culture of continuous improvement and quality service delivery.\r\n\r\nUpon completion, participants will be able to:\r\n•	Understand the role and value of well-structured administrative procedures in operational efficiency.\r\n•	Map and document existing workflows accurately.\r\n•	Analyze and evaluate processes to detect inefficiencies, redundancies, and potential improvements.\r\n•	Design and draft optimized procedures and workflows aligned with organizational needs.\r\n•	Plan and manage the implementation of new procedures with stakeholder communication and change management.\r\n•	Promote and support a culture of continuous improvement and quality administration.\r\n•	Produce standard procedure documentation that enhances clarity, accountability, and service quality.', 'This course is ideal for:\r\n•	Administrative staff involved in workflow execution and documentation\r\n•	Team leaders, supervisors, and middle management responsible for operations or service units\r\n•	Quality assurance or process improvement officers\r\n•	Department heads seeking to standardize procedures and enhance organizational efficiency\r\n•	New employees in administrative/operational roles who need clarity on institutional workflows', 'Day 1 – Foundations of Administrative Procedures & Process Mapping\r\n•	Introduction: definition and role of procedures in administration\r\n•	Principles of effective workflow design and procedure documentation\r\n•	Introduction to process mapping and flowcharting techniques\r\nDay 2 – Workflow Analysis & Identifying Inefficiencies\r\n•	Techniques for workflow / process analysis (bottlenecks, redundancies, delays)\r\n•	Tools: SIPOC, Value Add vs. Non-Value Add analysis, Lean / 5S overview\r\nDay 3 – Designing Improved Procedures (To-Be)\r\n•	Best practices in procedure design and standardization\r\n•	Defining responsibilities, roles, documentation requirements, and decision points\r\n•	Drafting new workflow diagrams and procedure documents\r\nDay 4 – Implementation Considerations & Change Management\r\n•	Change management basics: communicating change, stakeholder engagement, training staff\r\n•	Risk identification and mitigation when changing procedures\r\n•	Quality assurance and compliance considerations for administrative processes\r\nDay 5 – Presentation & Consolidation / Action Planning\r\n•	Participants present redesigned procedures and implementation plans\r\n•	Feedback session and peer review\r\n•	Consolidation: Establishing monitoring & evaluation mechanisms; continuous improvement cycle\r\n•	Final discussion, wrap up, and issuing of completion certificates'),
(2708, '25-02-163-M2708', 99, 163, 5, 1, 10, '10', 1670.00, '2025-10-06', 2, NULL, 'For the invoice from Avenue to INFOSHIELD', 'For the invoice from Avenue to INFOSHIELD', 'For the invoice from Avenue to INFOSHIELD', 'For the invoice from Avenue to INFOSHIELD'),
(2709, '25-02-109-E2709', 44, 109, 372, 3, 4, '260', 19500.00, '2025-12-05', 1, NULL, 'The HND Electrical Engineering program is an intensive 52 week applied engineering diploma designed to build advanced technical, analytical, and practical competencies in electrical and electronic systems. Modeled after the internationally recognized BTEC HND in Electrical & Electronic Engineering, this program prepares candidates for real world engineering roles across industrial, energy, and oil & gas environments.\r\nThe curriculum combines theoretical foundations with hands-on laboratory work, simulation environments, applied engineering projects, and supervised technical assignments. The program emphasizes power systems, electrical installation, automation and control, electronics, renewable energy integration, maintenance, and engineering project management.\r\nBy the end of the program, the candidate will be able to design, implement, test, troubleshoot, and optimize electrical systems with professional competence aligned with global engineering standards.', 'By the end of this training, participants will:\r\nDemonstrate advanced understanding of electrical and electronic engineering principles and applications.\r\nAnalyze, design, test, and troubleshoot electrical circuits, power systems, and control systems.\r\nInstall, maintain, and diagnose electrical and electronic equipment using engineering tools and safety standards.\r\nApply engineering mathematics, digital techniques, and technical drawing for project design and documentation.\r\nUnderstand power generation, distribution, and protection systems used in industrial facilities.\r\nEmploy modern automation and classic control techniques for industrial machinery.\r\nWork effectively in engineering teams, applying professional communication and technical reporting standards.\r\nComplete a final engineering project demonstrating applied knowledge and problem-solving capability.', 'Electrical technicians seeking advanced qualifications.\r\nEngineering assistants transitioning to full technical roles.\r\nOil & gas technical staff requiring deeper electrical competencies.\r\nMaintenance and operations personnel seeking structured engineering training.\r\nHigh school graduates or early career engineers aiming for professional development.\r\nIndividuals preparing for higher education pathways in engineering.', 'Module 1: Engineering Mathematics (Weeks 1–6)\r\nAlgebra, calculus, trigonometry\r\nVectors, matrices, complex numbers\r\nMathematical modeling for engineering\r\n\r\nModule 2: Electrical & Electronic Principles (Weeks 7–12)\r\nDC & AC theory\r\nKirchhoff’s laws, network theorems\r\nSemiconductor basics and electronic components\r\n\r\nModule 3: Electrical Installations & Safety (Weeks 13–18)\r\nWiring systems, protection devices, circuit breakers\r\nEarthing and bonding\r\nIndustrial electrical codes and safety standards\r\n\r\nModule 4: Engineering Drawing & CAD (Weeks 19–24)\r\nTechnical drawings and schematics\r\nElectrical CAD basics (AutoCAD Electrical)\r\nPanel layout design\r\n\r\nModule 5: Power Systems & Machines (Weeks 25–30)\r\nTransformers, generators, motors\r\nPower generation and distribution\r\nPower factor correction and load calculations\r\n\r\nModule 6: Classic Control Systems (Weeks 31–36)\r\nRelays, contactors, timers\r\nDOL, Star-Delta, reversing circuits\r\nInterlocking and safety circuits\r\n\r\nModule 7: Digital & Electronic Systems (Weeks 37–40)\r\nDigital logic, combinational circuits\r\nMicrocontroller introduction\r\nSensors and industrial interfaces\r\n\r\nModule 8: Maintenance Engineering & Fault Diagnosis (Weeks 41–44)\r\nPredictive and preventive maintenance\r\nCondition monitoring\r\nTroubleshooting techniques\r\n\r\nModule 9: Final Engineering Project (Weeks 45–52)\r\nProject proposal and approval\r\nElectrical design and construction\r\nTesting and performance validation\r\nTechnical report and presentation'),
(2710, '25-02-103-S2710', 59, 103, 373, 6, 7, '5', 1200.00, '2025-12-08', 1, NULL, 'A one week intensive program covering the essential modern technologies used to protect oil plants, including physical security, surveillance, fire protection, SCADA safety, cybersecurity, and emergency response.', 'Participants will learn to:\n\nUnderstand major risks in oil facilities\nApply modern security systems and surveillance tools\nOperate fire and gas detection technologies\nUse SCADA and IoT technologies for plant protection\nStrengthen cybersecurity of industrial environments\nManage emergency situations effectively', 'Engineers\nSafety officers\nSecurity personnel\nPlant operators\nFacility managers\nOil & gas professionals', 'Day 1 — Introduction & Physical Security Technologies\n\nTypes of oil facilities (refineries, terminals, pipelines, offshore).\nCommon threats and security challenges.\nModern physical security systems:\nAccess control & biometrics\nSmart perimeter fencing\nIntrusion detection sensors\nIndustrial security lighting\n\nDay 2 — CCTV, AI Surveillance & Drone Monitoring\n\nIndustrial-grade CCTV systems\nThermal & infrared cameras for oil sectors\nAI video analytics (intrusion, object tracking, anomaly detection)\nDrones & robotics for high-risk area inspections\nControl room integration & monitoring best practices\n\nDay 3 — Fire Detection, Gas Detection & Suppression\n\nFire hazards in oil facilities\nAdvanced detection technologies:\nFlame, gas, heat, laser smoke detectors\nFire suppression systems:\nFoam, water mist, dry chemicals\nSensor integration with SCADA & alarms\nNFPA standards related to oil & gas protection\n\nDay 4 — SCADA Safety, IoT Sensors & Cybersecurity\n\nSCADA architecture & real-time monitoring\nAutomated shutdown and control systems\nIoT sensors for operational protection\nCyber threats to ICS/SCADA networks\nNetwork segmentation & industrial firewalls\nIncident response planning\n\nDay 5 — Pipeline Security, Environmental Monitoring & Emergency Response\n\nPipeline surveillance:\nFiber optics, pressure monitoring, leak detection\nGPS asset tracking & remote isolation systems\nEnvironmental monitoring: toxic gas sensors, spill detection\nModern PPE & worker safety tech\nCrisis management & emergency communication systems\nFinal scenario-based exercise & course assessment'),
(2711, '25-02-147-C2711', 55, 147, 374, 5, 4, '5', 800.00, '2025-12-17', 1, NULL, 'In today’s competitive and rapidly changing business environment, organizations invest heavily in training and development. However, many training programs fail to deliver real value because they are not directly aligned with organizational goals and strategic priorities. This course is designed to bridge that gap by equipping participants with the knowledge and practical tools needed to design, implement, and evaluate training programs that directly support organizational performance and long-term objectives.\r\n\r\nParticipants will learn how to translate organizational strategy into targeted learning initiatives, ensure measurable training outcomes, and demonstrate the return on investment (ROI) of training activities. Through real-world examples, case studies, and hands-on exercises, this course transforms training from a cost center into a strategic driver of organizational success.', 'By the end of this course, participants will be able to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand the strategic role of training within organizational performance\r\nAnalyze organizational goals and translate them into learning and development priorities\r\nConduct effective training needs assessments aligned with business objectives\r\nDesign training programs that support strategic and operational goals\r\nAlign learning outcomes with key performance indicators (KPIs)\r\nMeasure and evaluate training effectiveness and impact\r\nDemonstrate training ROI and value to management and stakeholders\r\nEnsure continuous improvement of training programs', 'This course is designed for:\r\n\r\nTraining and Development Managers\r\nHR Managers and HR Business Partners\r\nLearning & Development (L&D) Specialists\r\nOrganizational Development Professionals\r\nTraining Coordinators and Officers\r\nDepartment Managers and Team Leaders involved in training planning\r\nConsultants involved in workforce development and capacity building', 'Day 1: Understanding Organizational Strategy and Training Alignment\r\n\r\nOrganizational vision, mission, and strategic goals\r\nThe strategic role of training and development\r\nCommon gaps between training and organizational objectives\r\nTraining as a driver of performance and competitiveness\r\nCase study: Successful alignment of training with strategy\r\n\r\nDay 2: Training Needs Analysis Linked to Organizational Goals\r\n\r\nIdentifying performance gaps vs. skill gaps\r\nOrganizational, job, and individual training needs analysis\r\nLinking training needs to strategic priorities\r\nStakeholder involvement in training planning\r\nPractical exercise: Conducting a strategy-driven training needs assessment\r\n\r\nDay 3: Designing Goal-Oriented Training Programs\r\n\r\nDefining measurable learning objectives\r\nAligning learning outcomes with organizational KPIs\r\nSelecting appropriate training methods and delivery models\r\nBlended learning and modern training approaches\r\nWorkshop: Designing a strategically aligned training program\r\n\r\nDay 4: Implementation and Performance Measurement\r\n\r\nEffective training implementation strategies\r\nChange management and learner engagement\r\nMeasuring training effectiveness (Kirkpatrick & Phillips models)\r\nLinking training results to business performance\r\nTools and techniques for monitoring and evaluation\r\n\r\nDay 5: Training Evaluation, ROI, and Continuous Improvement\r\n\r\nCalculating training ROI and value contribution\r\nReporting training results to management\r\nUsing data and feedback for continuous improvement\r\nAligning future training plans with evolving organizational goals\r\nFinal group project and presentations\r\nCourse review and action planning'),
(2712, '25-02-103-C2712', 59, 103, 375, 5, 5, '5', 1400.00, '2025-12-19', 1, NULL, 'In today’s competitive and rapidly evolving organizational environment, institutions are required to deliver high-quality services while optimizing resources and improving operational efficiency. This technical training proposal is designed to equip participants with practical frameworks, tools, and methodologies to achieve service excellence and enhance institutional performance.', 'General Objective\r\n\r\nTo enhance participants’ capabilities in achieving service excellence and improving institutional efficiency through practical and measurable performance improvement approaches.\r\n\r\nSpecific Objectives\r\n\r\nBy the end of the program, participants will be able to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand core concepts of service excellence and institutional efficiency\r\nAnalyze service performance and identify operational gaps\r\nApply service quality improvement tools and methodologies\r\nOptimize institutional processes and resource utilization\r\nDevelop performance indicators aligned with strategic goals\r\nDesign practical service and efficiency improvement plans', 'Department and Section Managers\r\nQuality and Performance Improvement Officers\r\nService Delivery Supervisors\r\nMiddle and Senior Management Professionals\r\nProcess and Operations Managers', 'Day 1: Foundations of Service Excellence\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\nConcept and principles of service excellence\r\nDifference between quality management and organizational excellence\r\nService excellence frameworks and models (EFQM, ISO, international best practices)\r\nCustomer expectations and service value creation\r\n\r\nPractical Output:\r\nInitial assessment of current service performance\r\n\r\nDay 2: Service Quality Improvement and Customer Experience\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\nCustomer journey mapping\r\nService touchpoints and pain-point analysis\r\nManaging customer feedback, complaints, and satisfaction\r\nDesigning high-value and customer-focused services\r\n\r\nPractical Output:\r\nCustomer journey map for an existing service\r\n\r\nDay 3: Institutional Efficiency and Process Optimization\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\nInstitutional efficiency concepts and measurement\r\nProcess mapping and workflow analysis\r\nIdentifying inefficiencies, bottlenecks, and waste\r\nIntroduction to Lean principles and Six Sigma concepts\r\n\r\nPractical Output:\r\nProcess improvement recommendations\r\n\r\nDay 4: Performance Measurement and Continuous Improvement\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\nPerformance management systems\r\nKey Performance Indicators (KPIs) design and alignment\r\nLinking individual performance to institutional objectives\r\nContinuous improvement and innovation culture\r\n\r\nPractical Output:\r\nDraft KPI framework for a selected service or department\r\n\r\nDay 5: Implementation, Leadership, and Sustainability\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\nLeadership role in sustaining excellence and efficiency\r\nChange management and stakeholder engagement\r\nDeveloping institutional improvement action plans\r\nPresentation and review of group projects\r\n\r\nPractical Output:\r\nComprehensive service and efficiency improvement action plan'),
(2713, '25-02-101-E2713', 51, 101, 376, 3, 1, '130', 12955.00, '2025-12-23', 1, NULL, 'Modern oil and gas facilities rely heavily on the reliable operation of rotating equipment such as pumps, compressors, motors, turbines, and gearboxes. Any failure in these critical assets can lead to production losses, safety risks, environmental incidents, and high maintenance costs. Among the most common root causes of rotating equipment failure are misalignment and excessive vibration.\r\n\r\nThe “Alignment and Vibration” training course is a comprehensive, long-term professional development program designed to equip oil and gas employees with the knowledge and practical skills required to ensure equipment reliability and operational excellence. This 26-week program combines solid theoretical foundations with extensive hands-on practice, real plant case studies, and industry-accepted standards.\r\n\r\nParticipants will learn how to correctly perform mechanical alignment, measure and interpret vibration data, diagnose machinery faults, and apply predictive maintenance techniques to prevent failures before they occur. The course emphasizes real-world oil & gas applications, safety compliance, and condition-based maintenance strategies that support asset integrity and operational continuity.\r\n\r\nBy the end of the program, participants will be able to confidently analyze rotating machinery conditions, identify early warning signs of failure, and contribute effectively to improving plant reliability, reducing downtime, and optimizing maintenance costs.', 'By the end of this program, participants will be able to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand mechanical alignment principles for rotating equipment\r\nPerform shaft, belt, and pulley alignment accurately\r\nMeasure, analyze, and interpret vibration data\r\nDiagnose machinery faults using vibration analysis\r\nApply predictive maintenance strategies\r\nImprove equipment reliability and reduce unplanned downtime\r\nComply with oil & gas safety and operational standards', 'Mechanical Technicians & Engineers\r\nMaintenance & Reliability Engineers\r\nRotating Equipment Specialists\r\nCondition Monitoring Personnel\r\nPlant Supervisors (Oil & Gas Sector)', 'PHASE 1: Fundamentals & Safety (Weeks 1–3)\r\n\r\nWeek 1: Introduction to Oil & Gas Rotating Equipment\r\n\r\nOverview of oil & gas production facilities\r\nCritical rotating machinery (pumps, compressors, turbines)\r\nFailure modes and consequences\r\nIntroduction to reliability engineering\r\n\r\nWeek 2: Mechanical Fundamentals\r\n\r\nShafts, bearings, couplings, seals\r\nTypes of loads and stresses\r\nLubrication basics\r\nEquipment installation best practices\r\n\r\nWeek 3: Safety & Standards\r\n\r\nOil & gas safety regulations (API, ISO, OSHA concepts)\r\nLockout/Tagout (LOTO)\r\nHot work and confined space awareness\r\nAlignment & vibration safety precautions\r\n\r\nPHASE 2: Alignment Principles (Weeks 4–9)\r\n\r\nWeek 4: Alignment Theory\r\n\r\nWhat is misalignment and why it matters?\r\nTypes of misalignment (angular, parallel, combined)\r\nEffects on bearings, seals, and couplings\r\n\r\nWeek 5: Soft Foot & Base Conditions\r\n\r\nSoft foot types and detection\r\nBaseplate and foundation issues\r\nShimming techniques\r\n\r\nWeek 6: Couplings & Drive Systems\r\n\r\nRigid vs flexible couplings\r\nBelt drives and pulley alignment\r\nGear-driven equipment\r\n\r\nWeek 7: Alignment Tools & Methods\r\n\r\nStraightedge and dial indicator methods\r\nReverse dial method\r\nLaser alignment systems (theory)\r\n\r\nWeek 8: Practical Alignment Workshop\r\n\r\nShaft alignment exercises\r\nVertical & horizontal machines\r\nReal-world oil & gas scenarios\r\n\r\nWeek 9: Alignment Tolerances & Documentation\r\n\r\nAlignment tolerances (API recommendations)\r\nThermal growth considerations\r\nAlignment reports and documentation\r\n\r\nPHASE 3: Introduction to Vibration (Weeks 10–14)\r\n\r\nWeek 10: Vibration Fundamentals\r\n\r\nWhat is vibration?\r\nTime domain vs frequency domain\r\nUnits: displacement, velocity, acceleration\r\n\r\nWeek 11: Measurement & Sensors\r\n\r\nAccelerometers and proximity probes\r\nMounting techniques\r\nData collection routes\r\n\r\nWeek 12: FFT & Signal Processing\r\n\r\nFast Fourier Transform (FFT) basics\r\nSpectral analysis\r\nSampling, resolution, and windowing\r\n\r\nWeek 13: Vibration Severity Standards\r\n\r\nISO 10816 / ISO 20816 overview\r\nAlarm and trip levels\r\nBaseline creation\r\n\r\nWeek 14: Practical Vibration Measurements\r\n\r\nUsing vibration analyzers\r\nData collection on pumps & motors\r\nTrending and baseline comparison\r\n\r\nPHASE 4: Fault Diagnosis (Weeks 15–20)\r\n\r\nWeek 15: Unbalance & Misalignment\r\n\r\nVibration signatures\r\nPhase analysis\r\nCase studies from oil & gas plants\r\n\r\nWeek 16: Bearing Faults\r\n\r\nRolling element bearing frequencies\r\nLubrication-related vibration issues\r\nEarly fault detection\r\n\r\nWeek 17: Gearbox Vibration\r\n\r\nGear mesh frequency\r\nTooth defects and wear patterns\r\nOil & gas gearbox case studies\r\n\r\nWeek 18: Electrical & Resonance Issues\r\n\r\nElectrical faults in motors\r\nStructural resonance\r\nNatural frequency problems\r\n\r\nWeek 19: Advanced Diagnostic Techniques\r\n\r\nEnvelope detection\r\nOrbit plots and time waveform analysis\r\nCross-channel measurements\r\n\r\nWeek 20: Field Troubleshooting\r\n\r\nRoot cause analysis (RCA)\r\nCombined alignment & vibration cases\r\nShutdown vs online monitoring decisions\r\n\r\nPHASE 5: Predictive Maintenance & Reliability (Weeks 21–24)\r\n\r\nWeek 21: Condition-Based Maintenance (CBM)\r\n\r\nPreventive vs predictive maintenance\r\nMaintenance planning using vibration data\r\n\r\nWeek 22: Reliability-Centered Maintenance (RCM)\r\n\r\nFailure modes and effects analysis (FMEA)\r\nOil & gas reliability strategies\r\n\r\nWeek 23: CMMS & Reporting\r\n\r\nIntegrating vibration data into CMMS\r\nWork order generation\r\nKPI tracking\r\n\r\nWeek 24: Program Implementation\r\n\r\nSetting up a vibration program\r\nRoute-based vs online systems\r\nManagement reporting\r\n\r\nPHASE 6: Assessment & Certification (Weeks 25–26)\r\n\r\nWeek 25: Final Practical Assessment\r\n\r\nAlignment task on rotating equipment\r\nVibration data collection and analysis\r\n\r\nWeek 26: Final Examination & Project\r\n\r\nFault diagnosis project\r\nPresentation & evaluation'),
(2715, '26-02-109-C2715', 44, 109, 224, 5, 0, '5', 1500.00, '2026-01-15', 5, NULL, 'test', 'test', 'tewst', 'test testt test'),
(2716, '26-02-106-C2716', 62, 106, 379, 5, 5, '3', 1300.00, '2026-02-10', 1, NULL, 'The LEGAL TRANSLATION is a specialized, practice-oriented course designed for legal professionals working within the oil and gas industry. The program focuses on enhancing participants’ ability to accurately translate legal documents between English and Arabic, with particular emphasis on contracts, agreements, policies, and regulatory texts commonly used in the oil and gas sector.', 'BY THE END OF THE PROGRAM, PARTICIPANTS WILL BE ABLE TO:\r\n\r\n UNDERSTAND THE PRINCIPLES OF LEGAL TRANSLATION\r\n IDENTIFY COMMON LEGAL TERMINOLOGY USED IN OIL AND GAS CONTRACTS\r\n TRANSLATE LEGAL TEXTS WITH HIGH ACCURACY AND CONSISTENCY\r\n AVOID COMMON LEGAL TRANSLATION ERRORS AND AMBIGUITIES\r\n APPLY INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC LEGAL LANGUAGE IN TRANSLATION TASKS\r\n REVIEW AND EDIT LEGAL TRANSLATIONS PROFESSIONALLY', 'LEGAL DEPARTMENT STAFF\r\n IN-HOUSE LEGAL COUNSELS\r\n CONTRACT ADMINISTRATORS\r\n LEGAL OFFICERS AND COMPLIANCE STAFF\r\n BILINGUAL LEGAL PROFESSIONALS INVOLVED IN TRANSLATION OR REVIEW', 'DAY 1: FOUNDATIONS OF LEGAL TRANSLATION\r\n\r\n INTRODUCTION TO LEGAL TRANSLATION\r\n DIFFERENCES BETWEEN LEGAL AND GENERAL TRANSLATION\r\n CHARACTERISTICS OF LEGAL LANGUAGE (ENGLISH & ARABIC)\r\n LEGAL SYSTEMS AND THEIR IMPACT ON TRANSLATION\r\n COMMON LEGAL TERMS AND EXPRESSIONS\r\n PRACTICAL EXERCISES ON BASIC LEGAL TEXTS\r\n\r\n DAY 2: LEGAL TRANSLATION IN THE OIL AND GAS SECTOR\r\n\r\n OVERVIEW OF OIL AND GAS LEGAL DOCUMENTATION\r\n CONTRACT TYPES IN THE OIL AND GAS INDUSTRY\r\n TRANSLATION OF:\r\n\r\n  EXPLORATION AND PRODUCTION AGREEMENTS\r\n  JOINT VENTURE AGREEMENTS\r\n  SERVICE AND PROCUREMENT CONTRACTS\r\n HANDLING DEFINITIONS, CLAUSES, AND CONDITIONS\r\n TERMINOLOGY MANAGEMENT AND CONSISTENCY\r\n PRACTICAL TRANSLATION WORKSHOPS\r\n\r\n DAY 3: ADVANCED PRACTICES AND QUALITY CONTROL\r\n\r\n RISK AND LIABILITY IN LEGAL TRANSLATION\r\n TRANSLATING REGULATORY AND COMPLIANCE DOCUMENTS\r\n CONFIDENTIALITY AND ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS\r\n REVIEWING, EDITING, AND PROOFREADING LEGAL TRANSLATIONS\r\n COMMON ERRORS AND HOW TO AVOID THEM\r\n FINAL PRACTICAL CASE STUDY AND FEEDBACK SESSION'),
(2717, '26-02-106-C2717', 62, 106, 377, 5, 5, '3', 1300.00, '2026-02-10', 1, NULL, 'The rapid advancement of Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming administrative and managerial functions across industries, including the oil and gas sector. AI-driven tools now play a critical role in improving efficiency, accuracy, decision-making, and operational support within administrative departments.\r\n\r\nThis training program is designed to provide participants from oil and gas companies with practical knowledge and hands-on exposure to AI applications that enhance daily administrative tasks, reporting, communication, data handling, and managerial support functions.', 'By the end of this program, participants will be able to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand core AI concepts relevant to administrative and office environments\r\nIdentify AI tools applicable to administrative workflows in oil and gas companies\r\nUse AI to automate routine administrative tasks\r\nImprove reporting, documentation, and data analysis using AI tools\r\nEnhance decision-support and productivity through AI-assisted systems\r\nApply AI ethically and securely within corporate environments', 'This program is intended for:\r\n\r\nAdministrative Officers\r\nOffice Managers and Coordinators\r\nHuman Resources and Training Staff\r\nPlanning and Support Staff\r\nSupervisors and Junior Managers\r\nAny administrative personnel working in oil and gas companies\r\nNo advanced technical or programming background is required.', 'DAY 1: INTRODUCTION TO AI IN ADMINISTRATIVE WORK\r\nAI FUNDAMENTALS FOR NON-TECHNICAL USERS\r\n\r\nDefinition of Artificial Intelligence\r\nAI vs Automation vs Digital Transformation\r\nCommon AI technologies used in offices\r\n\r\nAI IN THE OIL AND GAS ADMINISTRATIVE ENVIRONMENT\r\n\r\nAdministrative challenges in oil and gas companies\r\nWhere AI adds value in administrative functions\r\nOverview of AI adoption in energy sector offices\r\n\r\nAI TOOLS FOR DAILY ADMINISTRATIVE TASKS\r\n\r\nAI-powered document creation and editing\r\nEmail drafting, summarization, and response automation\r\nScheduling, task management, and reminders\r\n\r\nDAY 2: AI FOR DATA, REPORTING, AND COMMUNICATION\r\nAI IN DATA HANDLING AND ANALYSIS\r\n\r\nUsing AI to organize and clean administrative data\r\nAI-assisted spreadsheets and reports\r\nBasic data visualization using AI tools\r\n\r\nAI FOR REPORT WRITING AND DOCUMENTATION\r\n\r\nGenerating technical and administrative reports\r\nMeeting minutes and executive summaries\r\nPolicy, procedure, and memo drafting\r\n\r\nAI-ENHANCED COMMUNICATION\r\n\r\nPreparing professional presentations using AI\r\nAI support for internal and external communication\r\nMultilingual translation and communication tools\r\n\r\nDAY 3: AI FOR DECISION SUPPORT AND ADMINISTRATIVE EXCELLENCE\r\nAI FOR DECISION SUPPORT\r\n\r\nAI-assisted decision-making concepts\r\nUsing AI insights for administrative planning\r\nScenario analysis and forecasting basics\r\n\r\nETHICS, SECURITY, AND DATA CONFIDENTIALITY\r\n\r\nData privacy considerations in oil and gas companies\r\nEthical use of AI in administrative work\r\nRisk management and compliance considerations\r\n\r\nPRACTICAL WORKSHOP AND CASE STUDIES\r\n\r\nRealistic administrative scenarios from oil and gas companies\r\nHands-on AI tool application\r\nGroup exercises and solution presentations\r\n\r\nFUTURE OF AI IN ADMINISTRATIVE ROLES\r\n\r\nEmerging AI trends in corporate administration\r\nSkills required for future administrative professionals\r\nBuilding an AI-ready administrative team'),
(2718, '26-02-103-M2718', 59, 103, 380, 1, 1, '5', 1600.00, '2026-02-24', 1, NULL, 'In today’s rapidly evolving business environment, organizations face increasing economic, technological, and competitive challenges. Advanced strategic management has become a critical requirement to ensure organizational sustainability, competitiveness, and long-term success.\r\n\r\nThis training program is designed to enhance leadership capabilities and enable participants to formulate, implement, and evaluate modern strategies using internationally recognized strategic management practices. The program focuses on moving organizations from operational thinking toward advanced strategic leadership and decision-making.', 'The program aims to enable participants to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand modern concepts of advanced strategic management.\r\nAnalyze internal and external business environments effectively.\r\nDevelop organizational vision, mission, and strategic objectives.\r\nDesign practical and implementable strategic plans.\r\nApply modern strategic analysis tools.\r\nManage strategic risks and organizational challenges.\r\nStrengthen strategic leadership and decision-making skills.\r\nMonitor and evaluate institutional strategic performance.', 'Senior Executives and Top Management.\r\nGeneral Managers and Department Managers.\r\nSection Heads and Supervisors.\r\nStrategic Planning and Organizational Development Officers.\r\nQuality Management and Institutional Development Personnel.', 'DAY 1: INTRODUCTION TO ADVANCED STRATEGIC MANAGEMENT\r\n\r\nEvolution of Strategic Management Concepts.\r\nOperational Management vs Strategic Management.\r\nCharacteristics of High-Performing Strategic Organizations.\r\nStrategic Thinking for Executives.\r\nGlobal Trends in Strategic Management.\r\n\r\nDAY 2: STRATEGIC ANALYSIS AND STRATEGY FORMULATION\r\n\r\nExternal Environment Analysis (PESTEL Analysis).\r\nInternal Organizational Analysis.\r\nAdvanced SWOT Analysis.\r\nStakeholder Analysis.\r\nBuilding Sustainable Competitive Advantage.\r\n\r\nDAY 3: ADVANCED STRATEGIC PLANNING\r\n\r\nDeveloping Vision, Mission, and Core Values.\r\nSetting SMART Strategic Objectives.\r\nStrategic Plan Development.\r\nKey Performance Indicators (KPIs).\r\nStrategic Roadmap Design.\r\n\r\nDAY 4: STRATEGY IMPLEMENTATION AND CHANGE MANAGEMENT\r\n\r\nTranslating Strategy into Operational Plans.\r\nOrganizational Change Management.\r\nTransformational and Strategic Leadership.\r\nStrategic Risk Management.\r\nManaging Resistance to Change.\r\n\r\nDAY 5: STRATEGIC PERFORMANCE AND GOVERNANCE\r\n\r\nBalanced Scorecard Implementation.\r\nStrategic Performance Measurement.\r\nCorporate Governance and Strategic Control.\r\nInnovation and Organizational Sustainability.\r\nDeveloping an Applied Strategic Action Plan.\r\n\r\n6. TRAINING METHODOLOGY\r\n\r\nThe program adopts an interactive and practical learning approach including:\r\nInteractive Lectures.\r\nCase Studies.\r\nGroup Workshops.\r\nPractical Exercises.\r\nOpen Discussions.\r\nDecision-Making Simulations.\r\nReal-World Organizational Applications.\r\n\r\nEXPECTED LEARNING OUTCOMES\r\n\r\nUpon completion of the program, participants will be able to:\r\nDevelop integrated strategic plans.\r\nAnalyze strategic challenges professionally.\r\nMake data-driven strategic decisions.\r\nImprove organizational performance and sustainability.\r\nLead teams toward achieving long-term strategic goals.\r\n\r\nEVALUATION METHODS\r\n\r\nPre-training and post-training assessment.\r\nEvaluation of practical activities.\r\nContinuous participant performance monitoring.\r\nFinal applied project assessment.\r\nParticipant satisfaction survey.'),
(2719, '26-02-115-M2719', 52, 115, 381, 1, 2, '5', 2100.00, '2026-03-12', 4, NULL, 'Personnel Affairs Management is considered one of the most important areas in any organization, as strategic policies play a crucial role in directing performance and enhancing work effectiveness. This workshop aims to review modern and contemporary trends in developing personnel affairs policies, with the objective of strengthening the committee’s ability to achieve the organization’s goals while improving employee satisfaction and performance.', 'To understand the concept of strategic personnel affairs policies and their importance.\r\nTo review modern trends in developing personnel affairs policies.\r\nTo analyze the challenges and opportunities faced by the committee in implementing personnel affairs policies.\r\nTo develop planning and implementation skills for personnel affairs policies.\r\nTo exchange experiences and knowledge among participants regarding best practices in human resources management.', 'Members of Personnel Affairs Committees.\r\nHuman Resources Managers and Officers.\r\nAdministrative and Executive Managers.\r\nPolicy Makers and Organizational Development Specialists.\r\nGovernment officials responsible for staff management and administrative affairs.\r\nSupervisors and team leaders involved in personnel management.\r\nProfessionals working in Human Resources and organizational governance', 'Day One: Understanding the Concept of Strategic Personnel Affairs Policies\r\n\r\nIntroduction to the concept of strategic policies and their importance in human resources management.\r\nAnalysis of the committee’s role in developing and implementing strategic personnel affairs policies.\r\nDiscussion of practical examples on how to design effective and sustainable policies.\r\n\r\nDay Two: Modern Trends in Developing Personnel Affairs Policies\r\n\r\nReview of the latest trends and practices in human resources management.\r\nDiscussion of the impact of technological and social developments on personnel affairs policies.\r\nStudy of international cases in adopting successful strategic policies for personnel affairs committees.\r\n\r\nDay Three: Analysis of Challenges and Opportunities\r\n\r\nAnalysis of the challenges faced by the committee in developing and implementing personnel affairs policies.\r\nExploration of opportunities available to improve performance and enhance employee satisfaction.\r\nDevelopment of strategies to overcome challenges and benefit from available opportunities.\r\n\r\nDay Four: Developing Planning and Implementation Skills\r\n\r\nDeveloping planning skills to design effective personnel affairs policies.\r\nCase studies on applying methods and tools in policy development.\r\nAnalysis of policy impact and evaluation, with adjustments made as necessary.\r\n\r\nDay Five: Exchange of Experience and Knowledge\r\n\r\nSession for exchanging experiences and knowledge among participants.\r\nReview of best local and international practices in personnel affairs management.\r\nDevelopment of an action plan to apply the lessons learned within the organization.'),
(2720, '26-02-101-M2720', 51, 101, 382, 1, 5, '5', 1650.00, '2026-04-26', 1, NULL, 'In the highly demanding and risk-sensitive Oil & Gas industry, effective administrative leadership is essential to ensure operational efficiency, safety compliance, and team performance. This program is designed to equip participants with practical leadership tools tailored to the dynamic environment of oil and gas operations.', 'By the end of this course, participants will be able to:\r\n\r\nUnderstand modern concepts of administrative leadership\r\nApply leadership styles effectively in industrial environments\r\nEnhance decision-making and problem-solving skills\r\nImprove communication and team management\r\nManage conflicts and workplace challenges professionally\r\nLead teams toward achieving operational and safety goals', 'Oil & Gas Sector Employees (Supervisors, Team Leaders, Engineers, Administrative Staff)', 'Day 1: Fundamentals of Administrative Leadership\r\n\r\nAdministrative Leadership Concepts\r\nThe difference between Management and Leadership\r\nLeadership styles and when to use them\r\nLeadership in Oil and Gas\r\n\r\nDay 2: Communication & Team Leadership\r\n\r\nEffective communication skills\r\nBuilding and leading high-performance teams\r\nMotivation techniques in industrial environments\r\nManaging multicultural teams\r\n\r\nDay 3: Decision Making & Problem Solving\r\n\r\nDecision-making models\r\nRisk-based decision making in Oil & Gas\r\nProblem-solving techniques\r\nCase studies from field operations\r\n\r\nDay 4: Conflict Management & Performance\r\n\r\nConflict resolution strategies\r\nHandling workplace stress and pressure\r\nPerformance management systems\r\nLeadership accountability\r\n\r\nDay 5: Strategic Leadership & Change Management\r\n\r\nStrategic thinking for leaders\r\nChange management in industrial organizations\r\nLeading during crises\r\nFinal workshop and assessment'),
(2721, '26-02-103-C2721', 59, 103, 383, 5, 6, '4', 1050.00, '2026-05-14', 1, NULL, 'This proposal outlines a 4-day theoretical training course on cathode protection (CP) for gas pipelines. The program equips participants with essential knowledge to understand, design, and monitor CP systems for corrosion prevention in gas pipeline infrastructure.', 'Participants will grasp corrosion fundamentals and CP principles specific to gas pipelines. They will learn to evaluate CP system designs, criteria, and monitoring techniques without practical implementation. By course end, attendees can assess CP effectiveness for compliance and maintenance planning', 'Engineers, technicians, and managers in oil and gas sectors involved in pipeline integrity and maintenance. Ideal for those seeking theoretical expertise in CP without prior hands-on experience. No prerequisites beyond basic engineering knowledge; suitable for up to 20 participants', 'DAY ONE:\r\n\r\nCorrosion Fundamentals & CP Basics	\r\nTypes of corrosion in buried pipelines; \r\nElectrochemical principles; \r\nIntroduction to sacrificial and impressed current CP.\r\n\r\nDAY TWO:	\r\n\r\nCP Criteria & Pipeline Surveys	Protection criteria (e.g., pipe-to-soil potentials); Survey methods; \r\nSoil resistivity evaluation.\r\n\r\nDAY THREE:\r\n\r\nSystem Design & Components	Anode selection, \r\nRectifier design, ground beds; \r\nCoatings integration for gas pipelines.\r\n\r\n\r\nDAY FOUR:\r\n\r\nMonitoring, Maintenance & Standards	Interference mitigation; \r\nRemote monitoring; \r\nIndustry standards (e.g., NACE, DNV); case studies.');
